__d(function(g,r,i,a,m,e,d){"use strict";Object.defineProperty(e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),e.createSrsState=function(n){return{cardId:n,repetitions:0,easeFactor:t,interval:0,nextReview:null,lastReview:null}},e.scheduleReview=function(t,o){const u=Object.assign({},t);o>=3?(0===u.repetitions?u.interval=1:1===u.repetitions?u.interval=6:u.interval=Math.round(u.interval*u.easeFactor*l[o]||1),u.repetitions+=1):(u.repetitions=0,u.interval=0);u.easeFactor=Math.max(n,u.easeFactor+(s[o]||0));const c=new Date;if(u.lastReview=c.toISOString().split('T')[0],u.interval>0){const t=new Date(c);t.setDate(t.getDate()+u.interval),u.nextReview=t.toISOString().split('T')[0]}else u.nextReview=null;return u},e.isDue=o,e.getDueCount=function(t){return t?Object.values(t).filter(o).length:0};const t=2.5,n=1.3,s={1:-.2,2:-.15,3:0,4:.15},l={1:0,2:1.2,3:1,4:1.3};function o(t){if(!t||null===t.nextReview)return!0;const n=(new Date).toISOString().split('T')[0];return t.nextReview<=n}},235,[]);
__d(function(e,t,a,i,r,o,n){r.exports=[{id:"01-001",topicNumber:1,front:"What are the six fiduciary duties owed to clients?",back:"OLD CAR: Obedience, Loyalty, Disclosure, Confidentiality, Accounting, Reasonable Skill and Care."},{id:"01-002",topicNumber:1,front:"Which fiduciary duties survive termination of the agency relationship?",back:"Confidentiality (forever in most states) and Accounting (until all transaction property is properly accounted for)."},{id:"01-003",topicNumber:1,front:"What is the difference between a client and a customer?",back:"A client is represented by the licensee and owed fiduciary duties. A customer works with the licensee but is NOT represented and is NOT owed fiduciary duties \u2014 only honesty, fairness, and disclosure of material facts."},{id:"01-004",topicNumber:1,front:"What are the three requirements for legal dual agency?",back:"(1) The state must permit dual agency, (2) the brokerage firm must permit it, (3) proper disclosure must be made AND written consent must be received from both buyer and seller."},{id:"01-005",topicNumber:1,front:"Which two fiduciary duties are impossible to fully deliver in dual agency?",back:"Undivided loyalty and full disclosure of confidential information. This is why informed written consent is mandatory."},{id:"01-006",topicNumber:1,front:"What type of agent cannot bind a client to a contract?",back:"A special agent (limited agent), such as a real estate agent or escrow officer."},{id:"01-007",topicNumber:1,front:"What type of agent may bind a client to a contract?",back:"A general agent, such as a property manager."},{id:"01-008",topicNumber:1,front:"How is express agency created?",back:"By spoken or written words \u2014 an explicit agreement between the parties."},{id:"01-009",topicNumber:1,front:"What is implied agency and why should it be avoided?",back:"Agency created by the actions of the parties rather than an explicit agreement. It should be avoided because it can create unintended fiduciary obligations and potential illegal dual agency."},{id:"01-010",topicNumber:1,front:"What is the difference between exclusive agency and exclusive right-to-sell listings?",back:"Exclusive agency: seller pays no commission if they find their own buyer. Exclusive right-to-sell: agent gets commission no matter who brings the buyer, including the seller."},{id:"01-011",topicNumber:1,front:"What is a net listing and is it legal?",back:"A compensation agreement where the broker keeps all proceeds above a specified sale price. It is illegal in most states because it creates a conflict of interest."},{id:"01-012",topicNumber:1,front:"What are the five ways an agency relationship can terminate?",back:"(1) Expiration of the agreement term, (2) Completion/performance (closing), (3) Force of law (bankruptcy, destruction, death), (4) Destruction of property or death of principal, (5) Mutual agreement."},{id:"01-013",topicNumber:1,front:"Does paying a commission create an agency relationship?",back:"No. Agency is created solely through the agency agreement, not through compensation."},{id:"01-014",topicNumber:1,front:"What is agency coupled with interest?",back:"An arrangement where the agent has ownership, security interest, or possession interest in the principal's property. It does NOT terminate upon death or incapacitation of the principal."},{id:"01-015",topicNumber:1,front:"What is ratification in agency law?",back:"Acceptance after the fact \u2014 either through signing paperwork or the parties' actions confirming an agency relationship that was already created."},{id:"01-016",topicNumber:1,front:"What is estoppel in agency law?",back:"A legal doctrine preventing one party from claiming no agency relationship existed when their actions or statements implied they agreed to one."},{id:"01-017",topicNumber:1,front:"What is a transaction broker (facilitator)?",back:"A non-agency role where the licensee assists without representing the consumer. The licensee does not exercise judgment or discretion and owes no fiduciary duties."},{id:"01-018",topicNumber:1,front:"What duties does a licensee owe to customers (non-clients)?",back:"Honesty, integrity, fairness, accounting for customer property, cooperation on showings, and disclosure of the agency relationship and material facts as required by law."},{id:"01-019",topicNumber:1,front:"What happens to a power of attorney when the principal dies?",back:"It terminates upon the death of the principal. It also terminates if revoked by the principal or if the principal divorces the spouse who held the POA."},{id:"01-020",topicNumber:1,front:"What must a licensee disclose when buying or selling property for themselves?",back:"Their professional status as a licensee. Any ownership interest in the property or family/organizational connection to the owner must also be disclosed."},{id:"01-021",topicNumber:1,front:"When must a licensee disclose their agency relationship to a customer?",back:"Before the third party reveals anything confidential."},{id:"01-022",topicNumber:1,front:"What is designated/appointed agency?",back:"When two separate licensees from the same brokerage firm each represent one party (buyer or seller) in the same transaction. Also called dual-license dual agency."},{id:"01-023",topicNumber:1,front:"Can a client unilaterally terminate an agency agreement?",back:"A client may revoke the relationship, but without good cause this likely constitutes a breach of contract. Mutual agreement is the proper way for both parties to terminate."},{id:"01-024",topicNumber:1,front:"What is the difference between a general and special power of attorney?",back:"General POA grants broad powers. Special POA is narrow in scope and authorizes specific transactions \u2014 similar to a licensee-client agency agreement."},{id:"01-025",topicNumber:1,front:"What is sub-agency?",back:"A traditional arrangement where a licensee brings an unrepresented buyer to a transaction and represents the seller. Not allowed in all states. Rare today because buyers typically have their own agents."},{id:"02-001",topicNumber:2,front:"What are the five elements of a valid contract?",back:"1) Offer and Acceptance (Mutual Assent), 2) Consideration, 3) Legally Competent Parties, 4) Legal Purpose, 5) Voluntary (Reality of Consent)."},{id:"02-002",topicNumber:2,front:"What does the Statute of Frauds require for real estate contracts?",back:"Real estate contracts must be in WRITING to be legally enforceable. Oral contracts for the sale of real estate are unenforceable in court."},{id:"02-003",topicNumber:2,front:"What is the difference between a void, voidable, and unenforceable contract?",back:"Void = no legal force, lacks a required element. Voidable = valid but one party may disaffirm. Unenforceable = may be valid but court won't enforce it."},{id:"02-004",topicNumber:2,front:"What is the difference between a bilateral and unilateral contract?",back:"Bilateral = BOTH parties make promises (most real estate contracts). Unilateral = only ONE party makes a promise (e.g., option contract)."},{id:"02-005",topicNumber:2,front:"What happens when a counter-offer is made?",back:"The original offer is TERMINATED. The counter-offer becomes the new offer. The original offer cannot be revived."},{id:"02-006",topicNumber:2,front:"What are the four remedies for breach of contract?",back:"1) Unilateral Rescission, 2) Partial Performance, 3) Suit for Damages (including liquidated damages), 4) Specific Performance (lawsuit to force performance)."},{id:"02-007",topicNumber:2,front:"What is specific performance and when is it used?",back:"A lawsuit to FORCE the breaching party to perform as promised. Common in real estate because each property is unique \u2014 money damages may be inadequate."},{id:"02-008",topicNumber:2,front:"What is the difference between an addendum and an amendment?",back:"Addendum = ADDS new terms not in the original contract. Amendment = CHANGES existing contract terms."},{id:"02-009",topicNumber:2,front:"What is the difference between assignment and novation?",back:"Assignment: transfer rights/duties to new party \u2014 original party remains liable. Novation: substitute new contract or new party \u2014 original party is RELEASED from obligations."},{id:"02-010",topicNumber:2,front:"What is consideration in a real estate contract?",back:"Something of legal value exchanged \u2014 the buyer's promise to pay the purchase price and the seller's promise to transfer title. Earnest money is evidence of consideration but is not itself consideration."},{id:"02-011",topicNumber:2,front:"When is a contract considered executed (fully performed)?",back:"When ALL parties have met ALL contract terms \u2014 delivery and acceptance of the deed AND payment of the purchase price. Before that, the contract is executory."},{id:"02-012",topicNumber:2,front:"What is an option contract?",back:"An offer to purchase specific real estate WITHOUT the obligation to buy. The optionee pays an option fee to the optionor. Exercising the option creates a bilateral contract."},{id:"02-013",topicNumber:2,front:"What is UETA?",back:"Uniform Electronic Transactions Act \u2014 gives electronic signatures the same legal weight as wet-ink signatures. States choose whether to adopt it; each party must agree to use e-signatures."},{id:"02-014",topicNumber:2,front:"What are the four most common real estate contract contingencies?",back:"1) Financing/Mortgage Contingency, 2) Appraisal Contingency, 3) Home Inspection Contingency, 4) Home Sale Contingency."},{id:"02-015",topicNumber:2,front:"What does 'time is of the essence' mean?",back:"Both parties must perform their responsibilities within the time limits stated in the contract. Missing deadlines can constitute breach."},{id:"02-016",topicNumber:2,front:"Can a licensee draft a real estate contract?",back:"No. Only a licensed attorney may draft real estate contracts. Licensees may fill in blanks on pre-approved forms but cannot draft contract language."},{id:"02-017",topicNumber:2,front:"What is liquidated damages?",back:"A pre-set amount of damages (often the earnest money deposit) specified in the contract that limits what the aggrieved party can collect if the other party breaches."},{id:"02-018",topicNumber:2,front:"When does binding acceptance occur?",back:"When the signed contract is DELIVERED back to the offeror. Acceptance occurs at signing, but binding acceptance requires delivery."},{id:"02-019",topicNumber:2,front:"What is rescission?",back:"Contract termination by operation of law (e.g., breach, destruction of the contract object). Parties are returned to their pre-contract positions."},{id:"02-020",topicNumber:2,front:"What is the 'mailbox rule' concept in contract acceptance?",back:"Acceptance is generally effective when dispatched (sent), not when received. However, most real estate contracts specify exact delivery methods in the notices clause."},{id:"02-021",topicNumber:2,front:"What makes a contract voidable?",back:"One party lacked legal competence (minor, mental incapacity), or there was mistake, misrepresentation, fraud, duress, or undue influence causing failure of reality of consent."},{id:"02-022",topicNumber:2,front:"What is a backup offer?",back:"An offer accepted by the seller that is contingent on the primary contract falling through. If the primary contract closes, the backup offer is terminated."},{id:"02-023",topicNumber:2,front:"What is severability?",back:"If a court deems one term in a contract unenforceable, the remainder of the contract remains enforceable."},{id:"02-024",topicNumber:2,front:"What happens if a buyer misses a contingency deadline?",back:"The buyer may lose their earnest money and be in breach of contract. The seller may be able to terminate and keep the earnest money as liquidated damages."},{id:"02-025",topicNumber:2,front:"What is the difference between a contingency and a clause?",back:"A clause is a general contract provision (time is of the essence, choice of law). A contingency is a specific condition that must be met or the contract may be terminated without penalty."},{id:"03-001",topicNumber:3,front:"What does LTV stand for and how is it calculated?",back:"Loan-to-Value ratio = Loan Amount / Property Value (sale price or appraised value, whichever is lower). A $160K loan on a $200K property = 80% LTV."},{id:"03-002",topicNumber:3,front:"When is PMI required and when must it be terminated?",back:"Required on conventional loans when LTV exceeds 80% (down payment < 20%). Auto-terminated when principal reaches 78% of original value. Borrower may request at 80%."},{id:"03-003",topicNumber:3,front:"What does PITI stand for?",back:"Principal, Interest, Taxes, and Insurance \u2014 the four components of a standard monthly mortgage payment."},{id:"03-004",topicNumber:3,front:"What is a discount point?",back:"1 point = 1% of the loan amount, paid at closing to permanently reduce the interest rate. On a $250K loan, 2 points = $5,000."},{id:"03-005",topicNumber:3,front:"What is the difference between a deed of trust and a mortgage?",back:"Deed of trust = 3 parties (trustor, beneficiary, trustee), title theory, non-judicial foreclosure. Mortgage = 2 parties (borrower, lender), lien theory, judicial foreclosure."},{id:"03-006",topicNumber:3,front:"What is a defeasance clause?",back:"Orders the lender/trustee to release full title to the borrower once the loan is paid in full. Prevents the lender from pursuing additional payment."},{id:"03-007",topicNumber:3,front:"What is an acceleration clause?",back:"Makes the entire debt due immediately upon borrower default. Lenders must send an Acceleration Letter before foreclosing."},{id:"03-008",topicNumber:3,front:"What is a due-on-sale clause?",back:"Also called an alienation clause \u2014 requires the borrower to repay the loan when transferring ownership to another party."},{id:"03-009",topicNumber:3,front:"What are the three types of conventional loans based on conforming status?",back:"1) Conforming \u2014 meets Fannie/Freddie limits, 2) Non-Conforming \u2014 doesn't meet guidelines, higher rates/PMI, 3) Jumbo \u2014 exceeds loan limits."},{id:"03-010",topicNumber:3,front:"What are the key features of an FHA loan?",back:"FHA-insured. 3.5% minimum down payment. MIP required for LIFE of loan (upfront + annual). Credit score 580+ for 3.5% down; 500-579 may require 10% down."},{id:"03-011",topicNumber:3,front:"What are the key features of a VA loan?",back:"VA-guaranteed for qualifying veterans. NO down payment, NO mortgage insurance, NO prepayment penalties. 181 days active duty or 3 months wartime service required."},{id:"03-012",topicNumber:3,front:"What is negative amortization?",back:"Occurs with some ARMs when the monthly payment fails to cover the interest due. Unpaid interest is ADDED to the loan principal \u2014 the loan balance INCREASES."},{id:"03-013",topicNumber:3,front:"What is a buydown?",back:"Pre-payment of interest at closing to TEMPORARILY reduce the interest rate. Example: 3-2-1 buydown reduces rate 3% year 1, 2% year 2, 1% year 3."},{id:"03-014",topicNumber:3,front:"What is the difference between the housing ratio and the debt ratio?",back:"Housing (front-end) ratio = monthly housing expense / income. Debt (back-end) ratio = total monthly debt obligations / income. Underwriters use both."},{id:"03-015",topicNumber:3,front:"What is a land contract (contract for deed)?",back:"Owner financing where buyer makes installment payments to seller. Seller retains title; buyer gets equitable title. Title transfers when paid in full."},{id:"03-016",topicNumber:3,front:"What is a promissory note?",back:"The borrower's written promise to repay the loan. A negotiable instrument \u2014 can be sold/transferred to another holder. Accompanied by a security instrument."},{id:"03-017",topicNumber:3,front:"What are the three Cs of underwriting?",back:"Credit (scores, history), Capacity (income, debt ratios), Collateral (property value via appraisal)."},{id:"03-018",topicNumber:3,front:"What is a fully amortized loan?",back:"Principal is paid down over the full loan term. Equal monthly payments; principal portion increases each month, interest portion decreases. Paid in full after the last payment."},{id:"03-019",topicNumber:3,front:"What is a balloon payment?",back:"A large lump-sum payment at the end of a partially amortized loan to pay off the remaining principal balance."},{id:"03-020",topicNumber:3,front:"How many points are on a $300,000 loan with $9,000 in points charged?",back:"3 points. $9,000 / $300,000 = 0.03 = 3%. Each point is 1% of the loan amount, so $9,000 in points on a $300K loan = 3 points."},{id:"03-021",topicNumber:3,front:"What type of foreclosure does a deed of trust with power of sale clause allow?",back:"Non-judicial foreclosure \u2014 the lender does NOT need to go through the court system to foreclose. The trustee handles the foreclosure."},{id:"03-022",topicNumber:3,front:"Who does PMI protect?",back:"The LENDER \u2014 not the borrower. PMI protects the lender against loss if the borrower defaults on a high-LTV conventional loan."},{id:"03-023",topicNumber:3,front:"What is a wrap-around mortgage?",back:"Seller financing where the seller's new mortgage wraps around their existing mortgage. Seller continues paying the first mortgage; buyer pays the seller."},{id:"03-024",topicNumber:3,front:"What is a reverse mortgage designed for?",back:"Older homeowners who want to use their home equity to stay in their homes. The lender makes payments to the homeowner instead of the other way around."},{id:"03-025",topicNumber:3,front:"What is APR and how does it differ from the interest rate?",back:"Annual Percentage Rate \u2014 includes the interest rate PLUS other loan fees. It is a more complete measure of the true cost of borrowing than the interest rate alone."},{id:"04-001",topicNumber:4,front:"What does MARIA stand for?",back:"Method of annexation, Adaptability for use, Relationship of the parties, Intention in placing, Agreement of the parties \u2014 the five tests for determining if an item is a fixture."},{id:"04-002",topicNumber:4,front:"What are emblements?",back:"Cultivated crops treated as PERSONAL property. A farmer is entitled to harvest crops even if the land is sold before harvest. Also called fructus industriales."},{id:"04-003",topicNumber:4,front:"What are the five rights in the bundle of rights?",back:"Possession, Control, Exclusion, Enjoyment, Disposition. These rights can be separated and sold/leased individually."},{id:"04-004",topicNumber:4,front:"How many square feet in an acre?",back:"43,560 square feet. Also: 1 square mile = 640 acres. 1 mile = 5,280 linear feet."},{id:"04-005",topicNumber:4,front:"What are the three legal description methods?",back:"1. Metes and Bounds (metes=direction/distance, bounds=physical features, Point of Beginning) 2. Lot and Block (recorded plat) 3. Rectangular Government Survey System (RGSS: meridians, baselines, townships, sections)"},{id:"04-006",topicNumber:4,front:"What is the priority order of liens?",back:"1. Property tax liens (always first) 2. Mechanic's liens (if work began before mortgage recorded) 3. First mortgage lien 4. All other liens by date of recording."},{id:"04-007",topicNumber:4,front:"General lien vs. Specific lien?",back:"General lien: affects ALL property (judgment, tax liens). Specific lien: affects only a specific property (mortgage, mechanic's lien, property tax lien on that property)."},{id:"04-008",topicNumber:4,front:"Easement appurtenant vs. Easement in gross?",back:"Appurtenant: attached to land, runs with the land, has dominant and servient estates. In gross: granted to a specific person/business (utility easement), no dominant estate."},{id:"04-009",topicNumber:4,front:"How is an easement by prescription created?",back:"Through continued, uninterrupted, obvious, EXCLUSIVE, and ADVERSE use without the owner's permission \u2014 similar to adverse possession but for use rights."},{id:"04-010",topicNumber:4,front:"How is an easement terminated?",back:"1. Express agreement 2. Abandonment (dominant estate takes action showing clear intent to stop) 3. Merger (one party buys the other \u2014 can't have easement on own land) 4. No longer needed."},{id:"04-011",topicNumber:4,front:"Riparian rights vs. Littoral rights?",back:"Riparian: MOVING water (river/stream). Littoral: STATIC water (pond/lake/ocean). Riparian owners of non-navigable water own to center of waterway."},{id:"04-012",topicNumber:4,front:"Fee Simple Absolute \u2014 what is it?",back:"The HIGHEST and most complete form of ownership. Full bundle of rights. Freely transferable, inheritable, indefinite duration."},{id:"04-013",topicNumber:4,front:"Fee Simple Determinable vs. Fee Simple Subject to Condition Subsequent?",back:"Determinable: 'as long as' \u2014 AUTOMATIC reverter; grantor has possibility of reverter. Condition Subsequent: 'on condition that' \u2014 must go to COURT to reclaim; grantor has right of re-entry."},{id:"04-014",topicNumber:4,front:"What is a life estate pur autre vie?",back:"A life estate measured by the life of someone OTHER than the life tenant. 'For the life of Rose.' Ends when that third person dies."},{id:"04-015",topicNumber:4,front:"Remainder vs. Reversion?",back:"When a life estate ends: Remainder \u2192 passes to a remainderman (third party). Reversion \u2192 returns to the original grantor or their heirs."},{id:"04-016",topicNumber:4,front:"What is waste in a life estate?",back:"Any act by the life tenant that significantly reduces the property's value. The remainderman or reversion holder can sue to stop waste and/or seek damages."},{id:"04-017",topicNumber:4,front:"Four unities required for joint tenancy (TTIP)?",back:"Time, Title, Interest, Possession \u2014 all owners acquired interest at the same TIME, from the same TITLE/deed, with equal INTEREST, and equal right of POSSESSION."},{id:"04-018",topicNumber:4,front:"What happens when a joint tenant sells their interest?",back:"The buyer becomes a TENANT IN COMMON with the remaining joint tenants. The unities of time and title are broken. The remaining joint tenants maintain joint tenancy among themselves."},{id:"04-019",topicNumber:4,front:"Right of survivorship \u2014 what is it and which ownership types include it?",back:"Deceased owner's interest passes automatically to surviving co-owners \u2014 bypasses probate and will. Found in: Joint Tenancy and Tenancy by the Entirety. NOT in Tenancy in Common."},{id:"04-020",topicNumber:4,front:"Tenancy by the Entirety \u2014 what are the key rules?",back:"Only for MARRIED couples. Both spouses must agree to sell or encumber. Protected from individual creditors of one spouse. Includes right of survivorship. Recognized in Maryland."},{id:"04-021",topicNumber:4,front:"Condo vs. Co-op?",back:"Condo: own unit in fee simple + share of common areas; separate tax bill per unit. Co-op: own SHARES in corporation that owns building; proprietary lease for unit; single tax bill."},{id:"04-022",topicNumber:4,front:"Timeshare estate vs. Timeshare use?",back:"Timeshare Estate: fee simple fractional ownership; can sell and inherit. Timeshare Use (Right-to-Use): only right to occupy for a time; no ownership; cannot inherit."},{id:"04-023",topicNumber:4,front:"What are the four leasehold estates?",back:"1. Estate for Years: fixed term, specific end date 2. Periodic Estate: auto-renews (month-to-month) 3. Estate at Will: either party can end anytime 4. Estate at Sufferance: holdover tenant, trespassing."},{id:"04-024",topicNumber:4,front:"What is Maryland ground rent?",back:"A unique MD arrangement where homeowner owns improvements but leases the land under a long-term ground lease (typically 99 years). MD law gives tenant the right to redeem (buy out) the ground rent."},{id:"04-025",topicNumber:4,front:"What are Maryland's transfer and recordation taxes?",back:"State transfer tax: 0.5% of consideration. Recordation tax: varies by county ($3.50-$5.00 per $500). Many counties add local transfer taxes. Both are standard MD closing costs."},{id:"05-001",topicNumber:5,front:"What does DUST stand for?",back:"Demand, Utility, Scarcity, Transferability \u2014 the four characteristics of value."},{id:"05-002",topicNumber:5,front:"What is the difference between market value and market price?",back:"Market value: most probable price in an open market (theoretical). Market price: what a buyer actually paid and seller accepted (actual)."},{id:"05-003",topicNumber:5,front:"What is the principle of substitution?",back:"A buyer won't pay more for a property than the cost of an equally desirable substitute. This is the basis of the sales comparison approach."},{id:"05-004",topicNumber:5,front:"What is the sales comparison approach?",back:"Compares the subject property to recent sales of similar properties (comps). Based on substitution. Most common for single-family homes. Adjust the COMPARABLE'S price."},{id:"05-005",topicNumber:5,front:"When is the cost approach used?",back:"For unique, non-income-producing properties like churches, schools, hospitals, theaters, and newly constructed homes. Value = land value (as if vacant) + depreciated improvement cost."},{id:"05-006",topicNumber:5,front:"When is the income approach used?",back:"For income-producing properties: apartments, shopping centers, office buildings. Uses GRM (1-4 units, monthly), GIM (5+ units, annual), or cap rate."},{id:"05-007",topicNumber:5,front:"How do you calculate GRM?",back:"GRM = Sales Price / Gross Monthly Rent. Used for 1-4 unit residential properties. Value = Monthly Rent x GRM."},{id:"05-008",topicNumber:5,front:"How do you calculate cap rate?",back:"Cap Rate = NOI (Net Operating Income) / Value. IRV triangle: cover the one you're solving for. I/R=V, I/V=R, R\xd7V=I."},{id:"05-009",topicNumber:5,front:"What is NOT included in Net Operating Income?",back:"Debt service (mortgage payments) and depreciation are NOT included in NOI. NOI = Effective Gross Income - Operating Expenses."},{id:"05-010",topicNumber:5,front:"What are the three causes of depreciation in appraisal?",back:"1. Physical deterioration (wear and tear) 2. Functional obsolescence (design/outdated features) 3. External depreciation / economic obsolescence (factors outside the property \u2014 ALWAYS incurable)."},{id:"05-011",topicNumber:5,front:"Curable vs. Incurable depreciation?",back:"Curable: cost to fix \u2264 increase in value (worth it). Incurable: cost to fix > increase in value (not practical). External depreciation is ALWAYS incurable."},{id:"05-012",topicNumber:5,front:"What is plottage?",back:"Increase in value from combining adjacent parcels into one larger parcel. Assemblage is the PROCESS of combining the parcels."},{id:"05-013",topicNumber:5,front:"Regression vs. Progression?",back:"Regression: higher-value property loses value near lower-value ones. Progression: lower-value property gains value near higher-value ones."},{id:"05-014",topicNumber:5,front:"What is reconciliation in an appraisal?",back:"Correlation of value estimates from different approaches. It is NOT an average \u2014 the appraiser gives more weight to the most reliable approach for that property type."},{id:"05-015",topicNumber:5,front:"What does FIRREA require?",back:"FIRREA (1989) requires that appraisals for federally related transactions must be completed by state-certified or licensed appraisers."},{id:"05-016",topicNumber:5,front:"What does AIR prohibit?",back:"Appraiser Independence Requirements prohibit lenders from attempting to influence or coerce an appraiser's opinion of value. Required for Fannie Mae loans."},{id:"05-017",topicNumber:5,front:"CMA vs. Appraisal?",back:"CMA: informal estimate by a real estate licensee (no legal standards, used for pricing). Appraisal: formal opinion of value by a licensed/certified appraiser per USPAP (used for lending)."},{id:"05-018",topicNumber:5,front:"In a CMA, if a comparable is inferior to the subject, what adjustment do you make?",back:"Adjust the comparable's price UPWARD. If the comparable is superior, adjust DOWNWARD. Always adjust the comparable, not the subject property."},{id:"05-019",topicNumber:5,front:"What is the principle of contribution?",back:"The value of an improvement is measured by what it ADDS to the overall property value \u2014 not its cost. The same improvement may add different value to different properties."},{id:"05-020",topicNumber:5,front:"What is the principle of anticipation?",back:"Value changes based on expectation of FUTURE events. Property near a proposed transit station may increase in value before construction even begins."},{id:"05-021",topicNumber:5,front:"What is bracketing in appraisal?",back:"Using both superior and inferior comparable properties to determine a probable range of values for the subject property."},{id:"05-022",topicNumber:5,front:"How does GIM differ from GRM?",back:"GRM uses gross MONTHLY rent (1-4 units). GIM uses gross ANNUAL income (5+ units, commercial). Both = Sales Price / Income."},{id:"05-023",topicNumber:5,front:"What is replacement cost vs. reproduction cost?",back:"Replacement: cost to build a functionally equivalent property with modern materials. Reproduction: cost to build an EXACT replica with same materials and deficiencies."},{id:"05-024",topicNumber:5,front:"What comps should be avoided in a CMA?",back:"Foreclosures, short sales, and other distressed sales should NOT be used as comps unless pricing for similar conditions. Comps must be arm's length transactions."},{id:"05-025",topicNumber:5,front:"What is Maryland's property tax assessment basis?",back:"Maryland assesses property at 100% of fair market value with reassessments every 3 years. The Homestead Tax Credit caps annual assessment increases."},{id:"06-001",topicNumber:6,front:"What does PETE stand for?",back:"Police Power, Eminent Domain, Taxation, Escheat \u2014 the four government powers over real property."},{id:"06-002",topicNumber:6,front:"What is eminent domain?",back:"Government's right to take private property for PUBLIC USE with JUST COMPENSATION. The process is called condemnation."},{id:"06-003",topicNumber:6,front:"What is escheat?",back:"When a property owner dies without a will and no heirs exist, the property reverts to the STATE."},{id:"06-004",topicNumber:6,front:"Eminent domain vs. Police power?",back:"Eminent domain: taking property \u2192 must pay just compensation. Police power: regulating use (zoning) \u2192 no compensation required."},{id:"06-005",topicNumber:6,front:"What is a variance?",back:"Permission to deviate from zoning due to UNIQUE HARDSHIP (not self-created). Area variance = dimensional relief. Use variance = different use."},{id:"06-006",topicNumber:6,front:"What is a nonconforming use?",back:"A legal use that no longer complies with current zoning. Grandfathered \u2014 can continue but cannot be EXPANDED."},{id:"06-007",topicNumber:6,front:"What is spot zoning?",back:"Rezoning a single parcel differently from surrounding properties. Generally ILLEGAL unless justified by public interest."},{id:"06-008",topicNumber:6,front:"What is a comprehensive plan?",back:"Long-term master plan adopted by local government. Zoning ordinances must be consistent with the comprehensive plan."},{id:"06-009",topicNumber:6,front:"What is a conditional use permit?",back:"Permission for a use allowed in the zone but requiring special review (e.g., school in a residential zone)."},{id:"06-010",topicNumber:6,front:"Deed restrictions vs. Zoning?",back:"Deed restrictions: PRIVATE limitations (enforced by HOA/neighbors). Zoning: PUBLIC regulations (enforced by government). Deed restrictions can be MORE restrictive than zoning."},{id:"06-011",topicNumber:6,front:"What does 'runs with the land' mean?",back:"A right or restriction that binds ALL future owners of the property, not just the original party. CC&Rs and easements appurtenant run with the land."},{id:"06-012",topicNumber:6,front:"What is CERCLA (Superfund)?",back:"Federal law imposing strict, joint, and several liability for hazardous waste cleanup. Current owners CAN be liable even if they didn't cause contamination."},{id:"06-013",topicNumber:6,front:"What is the lead-based paint disclosure rule?",back:"For homes built before 1978: must disclose known lead paint, provide EPA pamphlet, and give buyer 10 days to conduct risk assessment."},{id:"06-014",topicNumber:6,front:"What is inverse condemnation?",back:"When government regulation effectively TAKES property without formal eminent domain. Owner sues for compensation."},{id:"06-015",topicNumber:6,front:"What is a buffer zone in zoning?",back:"A transitional area between incompatible land uses (e.g., landscaping/setback between commercial and residential zones)."},{id:"06-016",topicNumber:6,front:"What is the Zoning Board of Appeals?",back:"Local board that hears variance requests, special permit applications, and appeals of zoning administrator decisions."},{id:"06-017",topicNumber:6,front:"What are building codes?",back:"Police power regulations setting minimum construction standards for safety, structural integrity, electrical, plumbing, and fire protection."},{id:"06-018",topicNumber:6,front:"Who enforces deed restrictions?",back:"HOA or neighboring property owners through court action (injunction). Government does NOT enforce private deed restrictions."},{id:"06-019",topicNumber:6,front:"What is a Phase I Environmental Assessment?",back:"A preliminary investigation of a property for potential contamination \u2014 reviews records, site inspection, interviews. Required for most commercial loans."},{id:"06-020",topicNumber:6,front:"What is the Chesapeake Bay Critical Area Act?",back:"Maryland law restricting development within 1,000 feet of the Chesapeake Bay and tributaries with buffer and impervious surface limits."},{id:"06-021",topicNumber:6,front:"What is a setback?",back:"The minimum distance a structure must be from the property line or street, as required by zoning ordinances."},{id:"06-022",topicNumber:6,front:"What authority does Maryland use for zoning?",back:"Maryland is a home-rule state \u2014 each county and Baltimore City has its own zoning code. There is NO statewide zoning."},{id:"06-023",topicNumber:6,front:"What happens to a nonconforming use if the structure is destroyed by fire?",back:"May lose grandfathered status if destroyed beyond a specified percentage (varies by local ordinance). Cannot be rebuilt as nonconforming in many jurisdictions."},{id:"06-024",topicNumber:6,front:"What are subdivision regulations?",back:"Local government rules governing how land can be divided into lots \u2014 require plat approval, streets, utilities, drainage."},{id:"06-025",topicNumber:6,front:"What is Maryland's lead paint requirement for rental properties?",back:"Maryland requires rental properties built before 1978 to be registered and inspected for lead paint \u2014 stricter than federal requirements."},{id:"07-001",topicNumber:7,front:"When does title transfer from grantor to grantee?",back:"Upon DELIVERY of the deed to and ACCEPTANCE by the grantee \u2014 NOT on the deed date or recording date."},{id:"07-002",topicNumber:7,front:"What are the essential elements of a valid deed?",back:"Grantor (competent), Grantee (identifiable), Consideration, Granting clause, Legal description, Signature of grantor, Delivery and acceptance."},{id:"07-003",topicNumber:7,front:"What is a general warranty deed?",back:"The best deed \u2014 warrants title against ALL claims from any prior owner. Contains 5 covenants. Full protection for buyer."},{id:"07-004",topicNumber:7,front:"What is a special warranty deed?",back:"Warrants only against claims arising during the GRANTOR'S ownership. Common in commercial and foreclosure sales."},{id:"07-005",topicNumber:7,front:"What is a quitclaim deed?",back:"Conveys whatever interest the grantor MAY have with NO warranties. Used for clearing title defects, divorce, family transfers."},{id:"07-006",topicNumber:7,front:"What are the 5 covenants in a general warranty deed?",back:"Seisin, Quiet Enjoyment, Against Encumbrances, Further Assurance, Warranty Forever. First 3 are PRESENT covenants; last 2 are FUTURE."},{id:"07-007",topicNumber:7,front:"Actual notice vs. Constructive notice?",back:"Actual: direct knowledge. Constructive: legal presumption because information is in public records (recording)."},{id:"07-008",topicNumber:7,front:"What is a race-notice recording statute?",back:"Later purchaser wins only if they paid VALUE, had NO notice of prior claim, AND recorded FIRST. Most common type."},{id:"07-009",topicNumber:7,front:"What is a chain of title?",back:"The complete recorded history of all ownership transfers for a property \u2014 examined during a title search."},{id:"07-010",topicNumber:7,front:"What does title insurance cover?",back:"Defects that existed BEFORE the policy date: forgery, undisclosed heirs, recording errors, missing signatures. One-time premium."},{id:"07-011",topicNumber:7,front:"Owner's vs. Lender's title insurance?",back:"Owner's: protects buyer for purchase price, lasts while they own. Lender's: protects lender for loan amount, required, paid by borrower."},{id:"07-012",topicNumber:7,front:"What is the Torrens system?",back:"Title REGISTRATION (government certifies ownership) vs. recording. Used in limited form in a few states including Maryland."},{id:"07-013",topicNumber:7,front:"What is a bargain and sale deed?",back:"Implies grantor owns the property but provides NO express warranties. Common in tax sales and foreclosures."},{id:"07-014",topicNumber:7,front:"What is probate?",back:"Court process to validate a will, pay debts, and distribute assets. Required for fee simple estates but NOT joint tenancy with survivorship."},{id:"07-015",topicNumber:7,front:"What is adverse possession?",back:"Acquiring title by ONACHO: Open, Notorious, Actual, Continuous, Hostile, Exclusive possession for the statutory period (MD: 20 years)."},{id:"07-016",topicNumber:7,front:"What is tacking in adverse possession?",back:"Successive adverse possessors can ADD their possession periods together to meet the statutory requirement, if in privity."},{id:"07-017",topicNumber:7,front:"Testate vs. Intestate?",back:"Testate: dying with a valid will. Intestate: dying without a will \u2014 state law determines distribution."},{id:"07-018",topicNumber:7,front:"What does the Marketable Title Act do?",back:"Extinguishes old claims and interests not re-recorded within a set period (typically 30-40 years), clearing old title defects."},{id:"07-019",topicNumber:7,front:"What is an acknowledgment on a deed?",back:"Notarization of the grantor's signature. Required for RECORDING but NOT for validity between grantor and grantee."},{id:"07-020",topicNumber:7,front:"What is color of title?",back:"A document that appears to give title but is legally defective. May give advantages in adverse possession claims."},{id:"07-021",topicNumber:7,front:"What does title insurance NOT cover?",back:"Defects arising AFTER the policy date, zoning violations, environmental contamination, and matters listed in the policy exceptions."},{id:"07-022",topicNumber:7,front:"What is Maryland's adverse possession period?",back:"20 years of actual, open, notorious, exclusive, hostile, and continuous possession. Requires clear and convincing evidence."},{id:"07-023",topicNumber:7,front:"What is Maryland's recordation tax?",back:"Tax on deeds and mortgages recorded in county land records; typically $3.50-$5.00 per $500 of consideration; varies by county."},{id:"07-024",topicNumber:7,front:"What is the covenant of seisin?",back:"Grantor warrants they OWN the property and have the RIGHT to convey it. A present covenant \u2014 breach occurs at delivery if false."},{id:"07-025",topicNumber:7,front:"How does Maryland intestate succession work?",back:"If spouse only: spouse gets all. If spouse + children: spouse gets 1/2, children share 1/2. No spouse: children, then parents, then siblings."},{id:"08-001",topicNumber:8,front:"What is a material fact?",back:"A fact that would affect a reasonable buyer's decision to purchase or the price they would pay. Must be disclosed."},{id:"08-002",topicNumber:8,front:"Latent defect vs. Patent defect?",back:"Latent: HIDDEN, not discoverable by ordinary inspection \u2014 MUST be disclosed. Patent: VISIBLE, buyer can see \u2014 generally no duty to disclose."},{id:"08-003",topicNumber:8,front:"What is the federal lead-based paint disclosure rule?",back:"For pre-1978 homes: provide EPA pamphlet, disclose known hazards, give buyer 10 days for risk assessment."},{id:"08-004",topicNumber:8,front:"What properties are EXEMPT from lead paint disclosure?",back:"Housing built 1978 or later, 0-bedroom units, leases under 100 days, lead-free certified housing, foreclosure sales."},{id:"08-005",topicNumber:8,front:"What is Megan's Law?",back:"Federal law for sex offender registries. Agents generally have NO duty to search or disclose \u2014 direct buyers to the registry if asked."},{id:"08-006",topicNumber:8,front:"What is puffing?",back:"Exaggerated opinion that a reasonable person would NOT rely on ('best house in town'). Not fraud. Different from false factual statements."},{id:"08-007",topicNumber:8,front:"What is active concealment?",back:"Actively hiding a defect (painting over water stains). This IS fraud \u2014 different from mere silence."},{id:"08-008",topicNumber:8,front:"What is fraud in real estate?",back:"Intentional false statement of material fact, made with intent to induce reliance, causing harm. Must be a FACT, not opinion."},{id:"08-009",topicNumber:8,front:"What is negligent misrepresentation?",back:"False statement made carelessly without verifying its truth. 'I think the roof is new' without checking."},{id:"08-010",topicNumber:8,front:"What is a stigmatized property?",back:"Property with psychological/perceived defects (murder, suicide, haunting, former meth lab). In MD, generally no duty to voluntarily disclose."},{id:"08-011",topicNumber:8,front:"Must agents disclose known material facts even if the seller objects?",back:"YES. Agents have an independent duty to disclose known material facts \u2014 they cannot hide defects just because the seller wants to."},{id:"08-012",topicNumber:8,front:"What is the Maryland Residential Property Disclosure and Disclaimer Statement?",back:"MD form sellers must complete for 1-4 unit residential properties, disclosing known defects in structure, roof, foundation, HVAC, etc."},{id:"08-013",topicNumber:8,front:"What is Maryland's disclaimer option for sellers?",back:"Sellers can opt for a disclaimer (selling 'as is') instead of full disclosure, typically with a buyer credit, but still must disclose latent defects."},{id:"08-014",topicNumber:8,front:"Does Maryland require disclosure of death, suicide, or homicide on a property?",back:"NO. Maryland law says these psychological stigmas are NOT material facts and need not be voluntarily disclosed."},{id:"08-015",topicNumber:8,front:"What happens if a lead paint disclosure is not provided?",back:"Civil penalties up to $19,000+ per violation, treble damages, and the buyer may have the right to rescind the contract."},{id:"08-016",topicNumber:8,front:"Can the 10-day lead paint inspection period be waived?",back:"YES. The buyer can waive the 10-day period in writing, or the parties can mutually agree to shorten or extend it."},{id:"08-017",topicNumber:8,front:"Flood zone disclosure: when is it required?",back:"When the property is in a Special Flood Hazard Area (SFHA) requiring flood insurance under FEMA maps."},{id:"08-018",topicNumber:8,front:"What HOA documents must sellers provide in Maryland?",back:"CC&Rs, bylaws, budget, reserve study, and rules. Buyers typically have a review and cancellation period."},{id:"08-019",topicNumber:8,front:"Is silence about a defect fraud?",back:"Generally NO, but silence about a KNOWN LATENT MATERIAL DEFECT can constitute misrepresentation."},{id:"08-020",topicNumber:8,front:"What is the agent's duty regarding lead paint?",back:"Ensure seller compliance \u2014 provide EPA pamphlet, sign disclosure form, give 10-day period. Agents need NOT inspect for lead paint."},{id:"08-021",topicNumber:8,front:"Does Maryland require mold disclosure?",back:"No specific mold disclosure law in Maryland, but known mold is a material fact and should be disclosed."},{id:"08-022",topicNumber:8,front:"What is a seller's duty when using the Maryland disclaimer option?",back:"Even with a disclaimer selling 'as is,' the seller must still disclose known LATENT MATERIAL DEFECTS. The disclaimer does not shield fraud."},{id:"08-023",topicNumber:8,front:"What is constructive knowledge?",back:"Legal presumption that the seller SHOULD have known about a defect. 'I didn't know' is not a defense if a reasonable person would have known."},{id:"08-024",topicNumber:8,front:"How does Maryland Condominium Act protect buyers?",back:"Requires delivery of public offering statement for new condos and resale disclosure packet for existing units, with buyer review/cancellation rights."},{id:"08-025",topicNumber:8,front:"If a buyer asks directly about a death on the property in Maryland, what should the agent do?",back:"Answer truthfully if the seller authorizes it, or state that the seller declines to answer. The law does not require voluntary disclosure but also does not authorize lying."},{id:"09-001",topicNumber:9,front:"Who does a property manager owe fiduciary duties to?",back:"The OWNER (principal), not the tenants. Includes loyalty, accounting, confidentiality, and reasonable care."},{id:"09-002",topicNumber:9,front:"What is an estate for years?",back:"A lease with a fixed start and end date. Terminates automatically \u2014 NO notice required."},{id:"09-003",topicNumber:9,front:"What is a periodic estate?",back:"Auto-renewing lease (month-to-month, week-to-week). Continues until proper notice given by either party."},{id:"09-004",topicNumber:9,front:"What is an estate at sufferance?",back:"Holdover tenant staying after lease expires without permission. Technically trespassing. Landlord may evict or accept rent."},{id:"09-005",topicNumber:9,front:"Gross lease vs. Net lease?",back:"Gross: tenant pays rent, landlord pays operating costs (typical residential). Net: tenant pays rent PLUS some/all operating costs (typical commercial)."},{id:"09-006",topicNumber:9,front:"What is the implied warranty of habitability?",back:"Landlord must maintain property in livable condition (heat, water, electricity). Cannot be WAIVED in residential leases."},{id:"09-007",topicNumber:9,front:"What is constructive eviction?",back:"Landlord's failure makes property UNINHABITABLE \u2014 tenant may vacate and terminate lease without penalty. Tenant MUST actually vacate."},{id:"09-008",topicNumber:9,front:"What is retaliatory eviction?",back:"Eviction in retaliation for tenant's complaints about code violations. ILLEGAL in Maryland and most states."},{id:"09-009",topicNumber:9,front:"What is self-help eviction?",back:"Landlord evicts without court (changing locks, shutting off utilities, removing belongings). ILLEGAL in ALL states."},{id:"09-010",topicNumber:9,front:"Normal wear and tear vs. Damage?",back:"Wear and tear: gradual, ordinary use (faded paint) \u2014 NO deduction. Damage: tenant-caused (broken window) \u2014 CAN deduct from deposit."},{id:"09-011",topicNumber:9,front:"Must landlords allow service animals in 'no pets' buildings?",back:"YES. Service animals and emotional support animals are reasonable accommodations under Fair Housing Act \u2014 cannot charge pet fees."},{id:"09-012",topicNumber:9,front:"What is Maryland's security deposit return deadline?",back:"45 DAYS after lease termination. Must include itemized list of any deductions and interest earned."},{id:"09-013",topicNumber:9,front:"What is the penalty for wrongful security deposit withholding in Maryland?",back:"Up to 3 TIMES the deposit amount. Landlord must provide written itemized damages within 45 days."},{id:"09-014",topicNumber:9,front:"What is Maryland's eviction notice for non-payment of rent?",back:"10-day notice to pay or quit. After 10 days, landlord can file a failure-to-pay-rent complaint with the court."},{id:"09-015",topicNumber:9,front:"What is Maryland's tenant right of redemption?",back:"In non-payment eviction cases, the tenant can pay ALL rent, late fees, and court costs before eviction and KEEP the premises."},{id:"09-016",topicNumber:9,front:"What does Maryland require for rental properties regarding lead paint?",back:"Pre-1978 rental properties must be REGISTERED with MDE and INSPECTED for lead paint. Provide certificate and EPA pamphlet to tenants."},{id:"09-017",topicNumber:9,front:"Does a leasing agent need a real estate license?",back:"YES. In most states (including MD), anyone who negotiates leases for others must hold a real estate license."},{id:"09-018",topicNumber:9,front:"What is a ground lease?",back:"Long-term lease (50-99 years) of LAND only. Tenant owns improvements but not the land. Maryland ground rent is a form."},{id:"09-019",topicNumber:9,front:"What is a triple net lease?",back:"Tenant pays rent + property taxes + insurance + maintenance. The most common net lease for commercial freestanding buildings."},{id:"09-020",topicNumber:9,front:"Fair Housing Act rental exemptions?",back:"Owner-occupied 4-unit or less, single-family home rented without an agent, religious organizations, private clubs. All have limits."},{id:"09-021",topicNumber:9,front:"What is the landlord's duty of quiet enjoyment?",back:"Tenant has right to possess and use the property without interference from landlord or anyone claiming superior title."},{id:"09-022",topicNumber:9,front:"How must Maryland security deposits be held?",back:"In a Maryland bank escrow/trust account, separate from landlord's own funds. Must earn interest for tenant."},{id:"09-023",topicNumber:9,front:"What is a holdover tenant?",back:"A tenant who stays after the lease expires (estate at sufferance). Landlord can evict or accept rent, creating a new periodic tenancy."},{id:"09-024",topicNumber:9,front:"What does Maryland's tenant-friendly redemption right allow?",back:"In non-payment cases, the tenant can pay all amounts due (rent + fees + court costs) before the eviction date and keep the premises."},{id:"09-025",topicNumber:9,front:"What is the typical property management fee?",back:"8-12% of collected monthly rent, though terms vary by agreement. Flat fees or different structures are also used."},{id:"10-001",topicNumber:10,front:"What are the 7 federally protected classes under the Fair Housing Act?",back:"Race, Color, Religion, National Origin, Sex (including gender identity), Disability, Familial Status."},{id:"10-002",topicNumber:10,front:"What is blockbusting?",back:"Inducing panic selling by claiming a protected class is moving into the neighborhood. ILLEGAL under Fair Housing."},{id:"10-003",topicNumber:10,front:"What is steering?",back:"Channeling buyers to or away from neighborhoods based on their protected class. ILLEGAL."},{id:"10-004",topicNumber:10,front:"What is redlining?",back:"Refusing to lend or insure in certain geographic areas based on neighborhood demographics. Prohibited."},{id:"10-005",topicNumber:10,front:"What is price-fixing?",back:"Competitors agreeing on commission rates or fees. PER SE illegal under Sherman Antitrust Act."},{id:"10-006",topicNumber:10,front:"What is a group boycott?",back:"Competitors agreeing to refuse to deal with a specific person or business. Antitrust violation."},{id:"10-007",topicNumber:10,front:"What is a blind ad?",back:"An ad that doesn't disclose the advertiser is a licensed real estate agent/brokerage. PROHIBITED."},{id:"10-008",topicNumber:10,front:"What is dual agency?",back:"One broker/agent representing both buyer and seller. Requires WRITTEN INFORMED CONSENT from both parties."},{id:"10-009",topicNumber:10,front:"What is designated agency?",back:"Brokerage assigns separate agents to buyer and seller. Each owes full fiduciary duties to their client. Avoids dual agency."},{id:"10-010",topicNumber:10,front:"Fair Housing Act exemptions?",back:"Owner-occupied 4-unit or less, single-family without an agent, religious organizations, private clubs, 55+ senior housing."},{id:"10-011",topicNumber:10,front:"Do Fair Housing exemptions apply to advertising?",back:"NO. All real estate advertising must comply with Fair Housing regardless of exemptions."},{id:"10-012",topicNumber:10,front:"What is the Maryland agency disclosure form?",back:"\"Understanding Whom Real Estate Agents Represent\" \u2014 must be presented at first scheduled face-to-face contact."},{id:"10-013",topicNumber:10,front:"What is the Maryland Real Estate Guaranty Fund?",back:"Reimburses consumers for financial loss from licensee misconduct. Max $25,000 per claim, $50,000 per licensee. Funded by license fees."},{id:"10-014",topicNumber:10,front:"What protected class does Maryland add beyond federal law?",back:"MARITAL STATUS. Maryland fair housing law protects 8 classes: the 7 federal classes plus marital status."},{id:"10-015",topicNumber:10,front:"What is MREC?",back:"Maryland Real Estate Commission \u2014 regulates real estate licensees, sets education requirements, investigates complaints, disciplines violators."},{id:"10-016",topicNumber:10,front:"What are Maryland's CE requirements?",back:"15 hours every 2 years, including 3 hours ethics, 1.5 hours fair housing, and 1.5 hours agency."},{id:"10-017",topicNumber:10,front:"What are Maryland's salesperson license requirements?",back:"60 hours pre-licensing education, pass state exam, 18+ years old, high school diploma, find sponsoring broker."},{id:"10-018",topicNumber:10,front:"What does Maryland require in ads?",back:"Name of the LICENSED BROKERAGE must appear in a reasonably prominent manner. Agent name alone is not sufficient."},{id:"10-019",topicNumber:10,front:"What is a tie-in arrangement?",back:"Requiring the use of a specific service (title, mortgage) as a condition of sale. May violate antitrust and RESPA."},{id:"10-020",topicNumber:10,front:"What is market allocation?",back:"Competitors dividing market by territory or property type. Per se illegal under Sherman Act."},{id:"10-021",topicNumber:10,front:"What disability accommodations are required under Fair Housing?",back:"Reasonable modifications (tenant pays) and reasonable accommodations (no fee for service animals, reserved parking)."},{id:"10-022",topicNumber:10,front:"What are the design/construction requirements under Fair Housing?",back:"Multifamily (4+ units) built after March 1991 must have accessible entrances, common areas, doors, and routes."},{id:"10-023",topicNumber:10,front:"Who enforces federal Fair Housing laws?",back:"HUD (Department of Housing and Urban Development). Penalties up to $21,000+ for first violation."},{id:"10-024",topicNumber:10,front:"What is the Do Not Call Registry?",back:"National registry; agents must check before cold-calling. Exceptions: existing clients (18 months), prospects (3 months)."},{id:"10-025",topicNumber:10,front:"What are Maryland's broker license requirements?",back:"3 years active salesperson experience, 135 hours additional education, pass broker exam, 18+ years old."},{id:"11-001",topicNumber:11,front:"How many square feet in an acre?",back:"43,560 square feet. Also: 1 square mile = 640 acres. 1 mile = 5,280 linear feet."},{id:"11-002",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate property tax using a mill rate?",back:"Mill Rate / 1,000 x Assessed Value = Annual Tax. 20 mills = $20 per $1,000 of assessed value."},{id:"11-003",topicNumber:11,front:"What is 1 mill?",back:"$1 of tax per $1,000 of assessed value. 1 mill = 0.001. 10 mills = 0.010."},{id:"11-004",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate prorated property tax (365-day method)?",back:"Annual tax / 365 = daily rate. Daily rate x number of days owed = prorated amount."},{id:"11-005",topicNumber:11,front:"For proration at closing, who owns the closing day?",back:"The BUYER owns the closing day. Seller's responsibility ends the day BEFORE closing."},{id:"11-006",topicNumber:11,front:"Accrued items (arrears) at closing \u2014 who gets debited?",back:"SELLER is debited for accrued items \u2014 they owe for time they owned but haven't paid yet."},{id:"11-007",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate a discount point?",back:"1 point = 1% of the LOAN amount (not sale price). 2 points = 2% x loan amount."},{id:"11-008",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate LTV?",back:"LTV = Loan Amount / Property Value (lower of sale price or appraised value). $160K loan on $200K property = 80% LTV."},{id:"11-009",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate commission?",back:"Sale Price x Commission Rate = Total Commission. Then apply co-broke split and agent split."},{id:"11-010",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate price per square foot?",back:"Sale Price / Total Square Footage = Price per Sq Ft. $300,000 / 2,000 sq ft = $150/sq ft."},{id:"11-011",topicNumber:11,front:"What is the formula for area of a triangle?",back:"1/2 x Base x Height. A triangular lot with 100' base and 80' height = 4,000 sq ft."},{id:"11-012",topicNumber:11,front:"What is Maryland's state transfer tax rate?",back:"0.5% of the consideration (purchase price). $300,000 sale \u2192 $1,500 state transfer tax."},{id:"11-013",topicNumber:11,front:"How is Maryland recordation tax calculated?",back:"Rate per $500 of consideration (varies by county, $3.50-$5.00). Round UP to nearest $500. Count increments x rate."},{id:"11-014",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate seller net proceeds?",back:"Sale Price - Mortgage Payoff - Commission - Transfer/Recordation Taxes - Other Closing Costs +/- Prorations = Net."},{id:"11-015",topicNumber:11,front:"What is the formula for assessed value?",back:"Market Value x Assessment Ratio = Assessed Value. MD uses 100% (no ratio needed)."},{id:"11-016",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate front-end (housing) ratio?",back:"Monthly Housing Expense (PITI) / Gross Monthly Income. Conventional cap: 25-28%."},{id:"11-017",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate back-end (DTI) ratio?",back:"Total Monthly Debt Payments / Gross Monthly Income. Conventional cap: 33-36%."},{id:"11-018",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate simple appreciation?",back:"Current Value x Appreciation Rate x Years = Increase. $250K x 4% x 3 years = $30K increase."},{id:"11-019",topicNumber:11,front:"What is the formula for perimeter of a rectangle?",back:"2 x (Length + Width). 100' x 150' lot = 2 x 250 = 500 feet perimeter."},{id:"11-020",topicNumber:11,front:"How many square yards in a 900 sq ft area?",back:"900 / 9 = 100 square yards. 1 square yard = 9 square feet."},{id:"11-021",topicNumber:11,front:"What is a special assessment?",back:"Additional tax for specific improvements (sidewalk, sewer) benefiting a property. Separate from regular property tax."},{id:"11-022",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate GRM and value?",back:"GRM = Sales Price / Monthly Rent. Value = Monthly Rent x GRM."},{id:"11-023",topicNumber:11,front:"How do you calculate cap rate?",back:"Cap Rate = NOI / Value. I / V = R. I / R = V. R x V = I. NOI excludes debt service."},{id:"11-024",topicNumber:11,front:"What is the key rule for Maryland recordation tax rounding?",back:"Round UP to the nearest $500 when calculating recordation tax increments."},{id:"11-025",topicNumber:11,front:"How many acres is 87,120 square feet?",back:"87,120 / 43,560 = 2 acres."},{id:"12-001",topicNumber:12,front:"What is the Maryland Real Estate Commission (MREC)?",back:"State regulatory body under MD Department of Labor governing real estate licensees \u2014 sets licensing standards, investigates complaints, and disciplines licensees."},{id:"12-002",topicNumber:12,front:"What are Maryland's salesperson pre-licensing requirements?",back:"60 clock hours from MREC-approved provider (including 3 hours ethics), pass PSI exam, 18+ years old, high school diploma, find sponsoring broker, $90 fee."},{id:"12-003",topicNumber:12,front:"What are Maryland's broker license requirements?",back:"135 clock hours, 3 CONSECUTIVE YEARS active salesperson experience (no breaks), pass PSI broker exam."},{id:"12-004",topicNumber:12,front:"What are Maryland's CE requirements?",back:"15 hours every 2 years, including specific mandatory topics (A: Legal Update, C: Fair Housing, D: Ethics, H: Brokerage Relationships). Topics A/D/H cannot be substituted with out-of-state courses."},{id:"12-005",topicNumber:12,front:"When must Maryland agency disclosure form be provided?",back:"At FIRST SCHEDULED FACE-TO-FACE CONTACT with an unrepresented party. For non-face-to-face first contact, disclose by same medium first."},{id:"12-006",topicNumber:12,front:"What is the Maryland agency disclosure form called?",back:"\"Understanding Whom Real Estate Agents Represent\" \u2014 covers seller's agent, buyer's agent, subagent, and dual agent."},{id:"12-007",topicNumber:12,front:"What is the Maryland Real Estate Guaranty Fund?",back:"Fund reimbursing consumers for financial loss from licensee fraud/misconduct. $25,000 per claim max, $50,000 per licensee aggregate."},{id:"12-008",topicNumber:12,front:"What is Maryland's security deposit return deadline?",back:"45 days after lease termination. Must include itemized deductions and interest. Wrongful withholding penalty: up to 3x deposit."},{id:"12-009",topicNumber:12,front:"What is Maryland's eviction notice period for non-payment?",back:"10-day notice to pay or quit. After 10 days, landlord files Complaint and Summons Against Tenant in Possession."},{id:"12-010",topicNumber:12,front:"What is Maryland's right of redemption for tenants?",back:"In non-payment eviction, tenant can pay ALL rent, late fees, and court costs before eviction date and KEEP the premises."},{id:"12-011",topicNumber:12,front:"What protected classes does Maryland add to federal Fair Housing (7 classes)?",back:"MARITAL STATUS, SEXUAL ORIENTATION, and GENDER IDENTITY \u2014 making 10 protected classes under Maryland law (7 federal + 3 MD additions)."},{id:"12-012",topicNumber:12,front:"What is Maryland's state transfer tax rate?",back:"0.5% of the consideration (purchase price). Counties add additional local transfer tax (typically 0.5-1.5%)."},{id:"12-013",topicNumber:12,front:"How does Maryland assess property for tax purposes?",back:"At 100% of fair market value (no assessment ratio). Reassessment every 3 years on county rotation."},{id:"12-014",topicNumber:12,front:"How is Maryland recordation tax calculated?",back:"Rate per $500 of consideration ($3.50-$5.00 varies by county). Round UP to nearest $500. Applies to deeds and mortgages."},{id:"12-015",topicNumber:12,front:"What is the Chesapeake Bay Critical Area Act?",back:"MD law restricting development within 1,000 feet of Chesapeake Bay and tidal tributaries. Buffer requirements, impervious surface caps."},{id:"12-016",topicNumber:12,front:"What is Maryland ground rent?",back:"Unique MD form: homeowner owns improvements but leases land long-term (99 years, renewable). Common in Baltimore. Tenant has right to redeem (buy out)."},{id:"12-017",topicNumber:12,front:"What is Maryland's condo buyer cancellation right?",back:"15 calendar days after receiving the public offering statement for new condo purchases."},{id:"12-018",topicNumber:12,front:"What is Maryland's Homestead Property Tax Credit?",back:"Caps annual assessment increases for primary residences, protecting homeowners from rapid tax increases due to appreciation."},{id:"12-019",topicNumber:12,front:"What are Maryland's lead paint requirements for rentals?",back:"Pre-1978 rental properties must be REGISTERED with MDE and INSPECTED. Provide lead certificate and EPA pamphlet to tenants. STRICTER than federal law."},{id:"12-020",topicNumber:12,front:"What happens if a Maryland agency disclosure form recipient refuses to sign?",back:"The licensee may SELF-CERTIFY on the form that disclosure was made. The form is NOT a contract."},{id:"12-021",topicNumber:12,front:"What notice can be used at Maryland open houses instead of individual forms?",back:"Open House Notice (MREC form) \u2014 must be posted conspicuously, at least 8.5x11 inches, in color, on MREC letterhead."},{id:"12-022",topicNumber:12,front:"How long does a Maryland salesperson have to apply for a license after passing the exam?",back:"1 year. License term is 2 years. If issued inactive, there's a 3-year window to affiliate with a broker."},{id:"12-023",topicNumber:12,front:"What is Maryland tenancy by the entirety?",back:"Married couple ownership: neither can sell/encumber without other's written consent, protected from individual creditors, right of survivorship."},{id:"12-024",topicNumber:12,front:"Who enforces Maryland fair housing laws?",back:"Maryland Commission on Civil Rights. Complaints must be filed within 1 year of the discriminatory act."},{id:"12-025",topicNumber:12,front:"What are Maryland's mandatory CE topics for experienced salespersons?",back:"Topic A: Legal & Legislative Update (3h), Topic C: Fair Housing (2h), Topic D: Ethics (3h), Topic H: Brokerage Relationships (3h), plus 4h electives = 15h total."}]},236,[]);
__d(function(_g,r,_i,a,m,_e,d){"use strict";function e(e){return e&&e.__esModule?e:{default:e}}Object.defineProperty(_e,'__esModule',{value:!0}),Object.defineProperty(_e,"default",{enumerable:!0,get:function(){return u}});var t=r(d[0]),o=e(r(d[1])),i=e(r(d[2])),n=e(r(d[3])),l=e(r(d[4])),c=e(r(d[5])),s=e(r(d[6])),f=r(d[7]);function u({onBack:e}){const[c,u]=(0,t.useState)(null);if(c){const e=s.default.find(e=>e.topicNumber===c);return e?(0,f.jsxs)(o.default,{style:y.container,children:[(0,f.jsx)(l.default,{onPress:()=>u(null),style:y.backLink,children:(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.backLinkText,children:"\u2190 All Topics"})}),(0,f.jsxs)(n.default,{contentContainerStyle:y.guideContent,children:[(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.guideTitle,children:e.topicName}),(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.overview,children:e.overview}),e.sections.map((e,t)=>(0,f.jsxs)(o.default,{style:y.section,children:[(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.sectionHeading,children:e.heading}),(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.sectionBody,children:e.body})]},t)),e.keyTerms.length>0&&(0,f.jsxs)(o.default,{style:y.keyTermsSection,children:[(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.keyTermsTitle,children:"Key Terms"}),e.keyTerms.map((e,t)=>(0,f.jsxs)(o.default,{style:y.keyTerm,children:[(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.keyTermName,children:e.term}),(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.keyTermDef,children:e.definition})]},t))]})]})]}):null}return(0,f.jsxs)(o.default,{style:y.container,children:[(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.title,children:"Study Guides"}),(0,f.jsx)(n.default,{children:s.default.map(e=>(0,f.jsxs)(l.default,{style:y.topicRow,onPress:()=>u(e.topicNumber),activeOpacity:.7,children:[(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.topicNumber,children:e.topicNumber}),(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.topicName,children:e.topicName}),(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.arrow,children:"\u203a"})]},e.topicNumber))}),(0,f.jsx)(l.default,{style:y.backBtn,onPress:e,children:(0,f.jsx)(i.default,{style:y.backText,children:"Back to Home"})})]})}const y=c.default.create({container:{flex:1,backgroundColor:'#fff',padding:20,paddingTop:60},title:{fontSize:24,fontWeight:'700',color:'#1a1a1a',marginBottom:16},topicRow:{flexDirection:'row',alignItems:'center',padding:16,backgroundColor:'#f8f8f8',borderRadius:10,marginBottom:8},topicNumber:{fontSize:16,fontWeight:'700',color:'#007AFF',width:28},topicName:{fontSize:16,flex:1,color:'#1a1a1a'},arrow:{fontSize:24,color:'#ccc'},backLink:{marginBottom:16},backLinkText:{fontSize:15,color:'#007AFF'},guideContent:{paddingBottom:40},guideTitle:{fontSize:26,fontWeight:'800',color:'#1a1a1a',marginBottom:12},overview:{fontSize:15,lineHeight:22,color:'#444',marginBottom:20,fontStyle:'italic'},section:{marginBottom:20},sectionHeading:{fontSize:17,fontWeight:'700',color:'#1a1a1a',marginBottom:6},sectionBody:{fontSize:14,lineHeight:22,color:'#444'},keyTermsSection:{marginTop:20,borderTopWidth:1,borderTopColor:'#e0e0e0',paddingTop:20},keyTermsTitle:{fontSize:18,fontWeight:'700',color:'#1a1a1a',marginBottom:12},keyTerm:{marginBottom:12,backgroundColor:'#f8f8f8',padding:12,borderRadius:8},keyTermName:{fontSize:14,fontWeight:'700',color:'#007AFF',marginBottom:4},keyTermDef:{fontSize:13,color:'#555',lineHeight:20},backBtn:{alignItems:'center',marginTop:12},backText:{color:'#007AFF',fontSize:15}})},237,[36,151,181,185,182,44,238,147]);
__d(function(e,n,t,a,r,i,s){r.exports=[{topicNumber:1,topicName:"Agency",overview:"Agency law governs the relationship between a real estate licensee and the parties they represent in a transaction. Thirteen percent of the Maryland PSI exam tests agency concepts. You must know the types of agency relationships, how they are created and terminated, the fiduciary duties owed to clients (OLD CAR), the duties owed to customers, and the disclosure requirements. Maryland has specific agency disclosure rules that differ from general common law \u2014 pay close attention to the Maryland supplement below.",sections:[{heading:"A. Agency and Non-Agency Relationships",body:"**Agency** is a legal relationship in which a licensee (agent) enters into a contract to represent a client (principal) in a real estate transaction in exchange for compensation. The key parties are:\n\n- **Consumer** \u2014 anyone who uses or purchases a product or service.\n- **Customer** \u2014 someone working with a licensee but NOT represented by that licensee. Not owed fiduciary duties.\n- **Client (Principal)** \u2014 the party represented by the licensee, owed full fiduciary duties.\n- **Agent (Fiduciary)** \u2014 someone in a position of trust who acts on behalf of another and owes loyalty.\n\n**Types of Agents:**\n- **Universal Agent** \u2014 broad authority to act for the principal (e.g., power of attorney holder).\n- **General Agent** \u2014 handles all dealings in a given area (e.g., property manager). May bind a client to a contract.\n- **Special Agent (Limited Agent)** \u2014 limited authority for specific tasks (e.g., real estate agent, escrow officer). Cannot bind a client to a contract.\n\n**How Agency Is Created:**\n- **Express Agency** \u2014 created by spoken or written words. All agency agreements should be in writing.\n- **Implied Agency** \u2014 created by the actions of the parties. Must be avoided \u2014 do not give expert opinions or perform agent-only tasks without an agreement.\n- **Ratification** \u2014 acceptance after the fact by signing an agreement or through the parties' actions.\n- **Estoppel** \u2014 prevents a party from denying an agency relationship because their actions implied agreement."},{heading:"A.2 Types of Agency Relationships",body:"**Single Agency:** One licensee represents one party (buyer OR seller). Single agency firms prohibit dual agency even if state law permits it.\n\n**Dual Agency:** Representation of both buyer and seller in the same transaction. Requires: (1) state law permits it, (2) brokerage permits it, (3) proper disclosure AND written consent from both parties. Two forms:\n- **Single-License Dual Agency** \u2014 one licensee represents both parties.\n- **Designated/Appointed Agency (Dual-License Dual Agency)** \u2014 two separate licensees from the same firm each represent one party.\n\n**Dual Agency Problem:** The agent owes fiduciary duties to both clients, but two duties are impossible to fully deliver: (1) **undivided loyalty** and (2) **full disclosure** of confidential information. This is why informed written consent is mandatory.\n\n**Sub-Agency:** A licensee brings an unrepresented buyer to a transaction and represents the seller. Not all states allow it. Today rare because buyers typically have their own agents.\n\n**Non-Agency Relationships (Transaction Broker / Facilitator):** The licensee does NOT represent the consumer in an agency capacity. Provides assistance without exercising judgment or discretion. State statutes govern these duties. Terms are used interchangeably across states."},{heading:"A.3 Types of Listing Contracts",body:"**Exclusive Agency Listing:** Only one brokerage markets the property. If the seller finds their own buyer, no commission is owed to the brokerage.\n\n**Exclusive Right-to-Sell Listing:** The listing agent earns a commission NO MATTER WHO brings the buyer \u2014 including if the seller finds the buyer themselves. Must have definite start and end dates. This is the most common listing type.\n\n**Open Listing:** Seller may work with multiple agents. Only the agent who successfully brings the buyer gets paid. No exclusivity.\n\n**Net Listing:** NOT actually a listing type \u2014 it is a compensation agreement where the broker keeps all proceeds above a specified sale price. Illegal in most states because it creates a conflict of interest and encourages unethical behavior.\n\n**Listing Agreement Essentials:** Must be in writing, include definite end dates, document both licensee and seller duties, and specify MLS/internet/yard sign marketing permissions."},{heading:"A.4 Buyer Representation Contracts",body:"**Exclusive Right-to-Represent:** Only one agent represents the buyer during the term. Other agents cannot represent the buyer.\n\n**Non-Exclusive / Open Buyer Representation:** Buyer may work with many agents. If the buyer enters an exclusive agreement, any open agreements are terminated.\n\n**Buyer Agreement Contents:** Compensation terms, parties' obligations, general property description (price, location, size). Agent duties: show properties, assist negotiations, notify of material changes, provide guidance. Buyer duties: authorize agent to locate and show properties."},{heading:"A.5 Powers of Attorney",body:"A **Power of Attorney (POA)** is a legal document granting rights and powers from a principal to an agent (attorney-in-fact) to make decisions on the principal's behalf. Two types:\n- **General POA** \u2014 broad powers to act on the principal's behalf.\n- **Special POA** \u2014 narrow scope, authorizes specific transactions (similar to a licensee-client agency agreement).\n\n**Important for Licensees:** Many brokers avoid POA situations due to conflicts of interest. If used, there must be a paper trail. Usually limited to signing at closing if the client cannot attend. A POA terminates upon the principal's death, revocation, or divorce (if granted to a former spouse)."},{heading:"A.6 Termination of Agency",body:"Agency agreements terminate in five basic ways:\n1. **Expiration** \u2014 the agreement's term ends.\n2. **Completion (Performance)** \u2014 the transaction closes.\n3. **Force of Law** \u2014 bankruptcy, property destruction, death, or incapacitation.\n4. **Destruction of Property / Death of Principal** \u2014 automatically ends the agency (except agency coupled with interest \u2014 see below).\n5. **Mutual Agreement** \u2014 both parties agree to terminate. Neither party can unilaterally terminate.\n\n**Additional Termination Events:**\n- Client may **revoke** the relationship. Without good cause = likely breach of contract.\n- Agent may **renounce** the relationship (duties may still apply).\n- Sellers terminating early may still owe commission.\n- **Duty of confidentiality extends beyond termination** \u2014 in most states, forever.\n- **Duty of accounting** ends when all transaction property is properly accounted for.\n\n**Agency Coupled with Interest:** The agent has an ownership or security interest in the principal's property. This special arrangement:\n- Continues until the interest in the property ends.\n- Death or incapacitation of the principal does NOT void it.\n- Is an exception to the normal termination rules."},{heading:"B. Agent Duties \u2014 Fiduciary Duties (OLD CAR)",body:"A licensee's fiduciary duties are owed to **clients only**, not to customers. The six fiduciary duties spell **OLD CAR**:\n\n1. **Obedience** \u2014 Follow the client's lawful instructions. Cannot obey illegal or unethical instructions.\n2. **Loyalty** \u2014 Put the client's interests ahead of your own and all others. Work in the client's best interests.\n3. **Disclosure** \u2014 Disclose all material facts relevant to the transaction. Material facts are any information that might impact the transaction.\n4. **Confidentiality** \u2014 Keep confidential any information that could harm the client's negotiating position (motivations, timeframes, willingness to accept different terms). Unless disclosure is required by law.\n5. **Accounting** \u2014 Proper handling of client property: keys, funds, paperwork, the property itself. No commingling or conversion of earnest money.\n6. **Reasonable Skill and Care** \u2014 Operate within your expertise. Advise clients to seek expert advice (attorney, inspector) on matters outside your knowledge.\n\n**What survives termination:** Confidentiality (forever in most states) and Accounting (until all property is settled).\n\n**Traditional Agency Duties (COALD):** The older common-law duties \u2014 Care, Obedience, Accounting, Loyalty, Disclosure \u2014 are the foundation for the modern six."},{heading:"B.2 Duties to Customers (Non-Clients)",body:"Licensees owe the following duties to customers (non-clients), even though no fiduciary relationship exists:\n\n- **Honesty, integrity, and fairness** in all dealings.\n- **Accounting** for any customer property handled.\n- **Cooperation** with other licensees on showings.\n- **Disclosure of agency relationship** \u2014 tell the customer who you represent before they reveal anything confidential.\n- **Disclosure of known material facts** as required by state law.\n\nCustomers are NOT owed fiduciary duties. This is the critical distinction tested on the exam."},{heading:"C. Agency Disclosures",body:"**Disclosure of Agency/Representation:** Licensees must disclose their agency relationship so all parties know who the licensee represents.\n\n**Disclosure of Conflict of Interest / Self-Interest:**\n- Licensees must disclose their professional status when buying/selling for themselves.\n- Must disclose any ownership interest in a property or family/organizational connection to the owner.\n- **Agency Coupled with Interest** must be disclosed \u2014 the agent's compensation depends on continuation of the agency."}],keyTerms:[]},{topicNumber:2,topicName:"Contracts",overview:"Contract law is the single most heavily tested topic on the Maryland PSI exam at 19% of questions. You must know the five elements of a valid contract, the Statute of Frauds requirement that real estate contracts be in writing, the distinctions between void/voidable/unenforceable, bilateral vs. unilateral contracts, option agreements, the offer-counter-offer-acceptance process, common contingencies, remedies for breach, and contract termination methods including assignment and novation. Contracts is the highest-leverage study topic \u2014 mastery here pays disproportionately on exam day.",sections:[{heading:"A. General Contract Law \u2014 Five Elements of a Valid Contract",body:"A valid real estate contract requires ALL five elements:\n\n1. **Offer and Acceptance (Mutual Assent)** \u2014 One party makes an offer; the other accepts it exactly as presented. A counter-offer is a rejection of the original offer and creates a new offer. Acceptance occurs when all parties have signed; binding acceptance occurs when the signed contract is delivered back to the offeror.\n\n2. **Consideration** \u2014 Something of legal value exchanged. The buyer's promise to pay the purchase price and the seller's promise to transfer marketable title via deed. Earnest money is evidence of consideration but is not itself consideration.\n\n3. **Legally Competent Parties** \u2014 Parties must have legal and mental capacity. Contracts with incompetent parties are voidable at the option of the incompetent party but enforceable against the competent party.\n\n4. **Legal Purpose** \u2014 The contract must result in a legal outcome. A contract for an illegal purpose is void.\n\n5. **Voluntary (Reality of Consent)** \u2014 Parties entered the agreement voluntarily with full understanding. Mistakes, misrepresentation, fraud, duress, or undue influence mean consent is not real and the contract may be voidable.\n\n**Key rule:** An oral contract for the sale of real estate is UNENFORCEABLE in court \u2014 even if it has all five elements. The Statute of Frauds requires real estate contracts to be in writing."},{heading:"A.2 Void, Voidable, and Unenforceable Contracts",body:"**Void Contract** \u2014 Not a contract at all. Lacks one or more requirements for validity. No legal force or effect. Example: contract for an illegal purpose.\n\n**Voidable Contract** \u2014 Appears valid but one or both parties have a legal right to disaffirm it. The contract is voidable by the incompetent/misled party but enforceable against the other party. Example: contract with a minor, or contract entered into under misrepresentation.\n\n**Unenforceable Contract** \u2014 May be valid but cannot be enforced in court. Example: oral contract for sale of real estate (Statute of Frauds).\n\n**Severability** \u2014 If a court deems one term unenforceable, the remainder of the contract remains enforceable."},{heading:"A.3 Bilateral vs. Unilateral Contracts and Option Agreements",body:"**Bilateral Contract** \u2014 BOTH parties make promises and have obligations. Most real estate contracts are bilateral. The purchase contract is bilateral: buyer promises to pay, seller promises to transfer title.\n\n**Unilateral Contract** \u2014 Only ONE party makes a promise. The other party is not obligated to perform. Example: an option contract.\n\n**Option Contract** \u2014 An offer to purchase specific real estate without the obligation to buy. Key features:\n- **Optionee** (potential buyer) pays an **option fee** to the **optionor** (seller)\n- If the optionee decides NOT to exercise the option, the optionor typically keeps the option fee\n- If the optionee exercises the option, the optionor MUST perform \u2014 the contract then becomes bilateral\n- A lease-with-option-to-buy is a common form\n- If the lessee violates lease terms while the option is active, the lessor may terminate the option"},{heading:"A.4 Executed vs. Executory Contracts",body:"**Executed Contract** \u2014 All parties have met ALL contract terms. A real estate sale contract is executed upon delivery and acceptance of the deed AND payment of the purchase price.\n\n**Executory Contract** \u2014 Some promises remain unperformed. A real estate sale contract is executory until the deed is delivered and accepted \u2014 even after both parties have signed, it remains executory until closing is complete."},{heading:"A.5 Offer, Counter-Offer, Delivery, and Acceptance",body:"**Offer Process:** Buyer makes written offer \u2192 Buyer is the **offeror**, seller is the **offeree**.\n\n**Counter-Offer:** Seller does not accept the original offer \u2192 Seller makes a counter-offer \u2192 Seller becomes the offeror, buyer becomes the offeree. A counter-offer TERMINATES the original offer \u2014 it can no longer be accepted.\n\n**Acceptance:** Occurs when all parties have signed. **Binding acceptance** happens when the signed contract is delivered back to the offeror. Both must occur within the stated time limits, or within a \"reasonable time\" if no deadline is specified.\n\n**\"Time is of the Essence\"** \u2014 Both parties must perform within the contract's time limits. If a buyer submits an offer with an acceptance deadline and the seller misses it, the offer is void.\n\n**Notices Clause** \u2014 Describes how contract-related notice may be made and when notice is considered received (e.g., electronic delivery)."},{heading:"A.6 Electronic Signatures (UETA)",body:"The **Uniform Electronic Transactions Act (UETA)** gives electronic signatures, records, and contracts the same legal weight as paper records with wet-ink signatures. Each state can choose whether to implement UETA; state law supersedes UETA when present.\n\n**Electronic Signature** \u2014 defined as \"an electronic sound, symbol, or process attached to or logically associated with a record and executed or adopted by a person with the intent to sign.\"\n\nIn many states, electronic documents and signatures can be used only if ALL parties agree."},{heading:"A.7 Breach of Contract and Remedies",body:"**Breach of Contract** \u2014 One party fails to meet contract obligations. Remedies available to the innocent party:\n\n1. **Unilateral Rescission** \u2014 The innocent party terminates the contract.\n2. **Partial Performance** \u2014 The innocent party agrees to accept partial performance, possibly with compensation.\n3. **Suit for Damages** \u2014 Monetary compensation for harm caused. **Liquidated damages** clauses limit buyer damages to return of earnest money if the seller breaches.\n4. **Specific Performance** \u2014 A lawsuit seeking to FORCE the breaching party to perform as promised. Used when money damages are inadequate \u2014 real estate is considered unique, so specific performance is common.\n\n**Liquidated Damages Clause** \u2014 Limits damages to a pre-set reasonable amount; in many purchase contracts, this is the earnest money deposit."},{heading:"A.8 Contract Termination: Discharge, Release, Rescission, Assignment, Novation",body:"**Discharge (Performance/Execution)** \u2014 All parties have met all contract terms and are released from obligations.\n\n**Termination** \u2014 One party ends the contract before full performance; the terminating party may owe duties or compensation.\n\n**Release** \u2014 One party agrees in writing that the other is no longer held to contract provisions.\n\n**Rescission (Cancellation)** \u2014 Results from operation of law (breach, destruction of the contract's object). Parties are returned to their pre-contract positions.\n\n**Mutual Agreement** \u2014 Parties agree to terminate; best accomplished through mutual written release.\n\n**Assignment** \u2014 Transfer of contractual rights or duties to a new party. Generally permitted UNLESS the contract specifically prohibits it. The original party remains liable if the new party fails to perform unless released.\n\n**Novation** \u2014 Substitution of a new contract for an old one, OR substitution of a new party. The intent is to DISCHARGE the old obligation and RELEASE the original party \u2014 this is the key difference from assignment."},{heading:"B. Purchase Contracts \u2014 Addenda, Amendments, and Contingencies",body:"**Addendum vs. Amendment:**\n- **Addendum** \u2014 ADDS new terms not addressed in the main contract form.\n- **Amendment** \u2014 CHANGES one or more existing contract terms.\n\n**Common Contract Clauses:**\n- \"Time is of the essence\" \u2014 performance must occur within stated time limits.\n- Choice of law \u2014 disputes determined under a specific jurisdiction's law.\n- Indemnification \u2014 one party compensates the other for harm or loss.\n- Contingency \u2014 a condition that must be met to avoid breach.\n- Arbitration \u2014 disputes resolved through arbitration.\n- Mediation \u2014 parties attempt mediation before arbitration or litigation.\n- Statute of limitations \u2014 timeframe for filing a lawsuit.\n- Liquidated damages \u2014 pre-set reasonable damages amount.\n\n**Common Contingencies:**\n- **Financing/Mortgage Contingency** \u2014 Sale contingent on buyer obtaining financing.\n- **Appraisal Contingency** \u2014 Property must appraise at or above the sales price.\n- **Home Inspection Contingency** \u2014 Sale contingent on satisfactory inspection.\n- **Home Sale Contingency** \u2014 Sale contingent on buyer selling their existing home.\n\nAll contingencies must be satisfied by their stated deadlines. Missing a contingency deadline = breach. If a buyer misses a deadline, they may lose earnest money. If a seller misses a deadline, the buyer may terminate. More contingencies = less attractive offer to the seller."},{heading:"C. Multiple Offers and Counter-Offers",body:"**Handling Multiple Offers:** Licensees must present ALL offers to the seller promptly. The seller may accept one, counter one, or reject all. The seller cannot accept multiple offers \u2014 only one contract can exist for the property.\n\n**Counter-Offer Rules:**\n- A counter-offer is a REJECTION of the original offer and creates a NEW offer.\n- The original offer cannot be revived \u2014 only the new counter-offer can be accepted.\n- If a counter-offer is rejected, the seller cannot go back and accept the original offer.\n- Both acceptance and binding acceptance must occur within stated deadlines.\n\n**Backup Offers:** A seller may accept a backup offer while the primary contract is pending. If the primary contract falls through (e.g., contingency not met), the backup offer becomes the primary contract. The backup offer must specify it is a backup."}],keyTerms:[]},{topicNumber:3,topicName:"Financing",overview:"Financing accounts for 10% of the Maryland PSI exam. You must know loan-to-value (LTV) ratios and PMI requirements, PITI payment components, points and loan fees, the underwriting process (housing and debt ratios), types of loans (conventional, FHA, VA, USDA), amortization concepts, security instruments (mortgage vs. deed of trust), promissory notes and key clauses, ARMs and negative amortization, owner financing methods, and key federal lending laws (RESPA, TILA, ECOA). Calculations are commonly tested \u2014 know how to compute LTV, discount points, and loan payments.",sections:[{heading:"A. Basic Financing Concepts \u2014 LTV, PMI, PITI, Points",body:"**Loan-to-Value Ratio (LTV):** The ratio of the loan amount to the property value (sales price or appraised value, whichever is LOWER). Formula: Loan Amount / Property Value = LTV. A $160,000 loan on a $200,000 property = 80% LTV. A 20% down payment = 80% LTV. Lower LTV = better interest rates and more loan options.\n\n**Private Mortgage Insurance (PMI):** Required on conventional loans when LTV exceeds 80% (down payment < 20%). PMI protects the LENDER, not the borrower. Must be terminated when principal reaches 78% of original value (automatic). Borrower may request termination at 80% LTV.\n\n**PITI (Budget Mortgage):** The most common mortgage payment includes:\n- **P**rincipal \u2014 portion of the loan balance paid down\n- **I**nterest \u2014 fee paid to lender for use of money; decreases over loan life\n- **T**axes \u2014 1/12 of annual property tax held in escrow\n- **I**nsurance \u2014 1/12 of annual homeowner's insurance held in escrow\n\nLenders place tax and insurance amounts in escrow and pay annual bills when due.\n\n**Interest Rate vs. APR:** Interest rate = cost of borrowing. APR (Annual Percentage Rate) = interest rate PLUS other loan fees \u2014 a more complete cost measure. Lenders typically lock in rates for up to 90 days.\n\n**Points:** One point = 1% of the loan amount.\n- **Loan Origination Fees (Loan Points):** 1-3% of loan; compensation for processing. Max typically 3%.\n- **Discount Points:** Paid at closing to permanently REDUCE the interest rate.\n- **Buydown:** Interest pre-payment to TEMPORARILY reduce rate (e.g., 3-2-1 buydown: 3% reduction year 1, 2% year 2, 1% year 3).\n\n**Key Security Instrument Clauses:**\n- **Defeasance Clause:** Orders lender to release full title when the loan is paid in full.\n- **Acceleration Clause:** Makes entire debt due immediately upon borrower default.\n- **Due-on-Sale (Alienation) Clause:** Requires loan repayment when ownership transfers.\n- **Prepayment Penalty Clause:** Charges for interest lost when a loan is paid off early (rare today)."},{heading:"A.2 Underwriting \u2014 Debt Ratios and Credit",body:"Loan underwriters analyze the 3 C's: **Credit** (scores, history), **Capacity** (income, debt ratios), and **Collateral** (property value via appraisal).\n\n**Housing Ratio (Front-End Ratio):** Monthly housing expense (PITI + second liens + association fees) divided by gross monthly income.\n- Conventional: typically 25-28%\n- FHA: typically 31-40%\n- VA: lenders ignore the front-end ratio\n\n**Debt Ratio (Back-End Ratio / DTI):** Total monthly debt obligations divided by gross monthly income.\n- Conventional: typically 33-36%\n- FHA: typically 43-50%\n- VA: typically cannot exceed 41%\n\n**Credit Scores:**\n- Conventional: typically 620+ minimum\n- FHA: 580+ for 3.5% down; 500-579 may require 10% down\n- Higher scores = better rates and terms; compensating factors (cash reserves, additional income) may allow higher ratios.\n\n**Credit History Factors:** Length of credit, late payments, available credit used, types of credit extended."},{heading:"A.3 Deed of Trust vs. Mortgage (Security Instruments)",body:"**Deed of Trust (Title Theory States):** Three parties involved:\n- **Trustor** = borrower\n- **Beneficiary** = lender\n- **Trustee** = independent third party who holds the deed\n\nLender/trustee holds LEGAL title until the loan is paid. Borrower holds EQUITABLE title (right to possess and use, right to obtain legal title on payoff). Includes power of sale clause enabling non-judicial foreclosure (no court required). At payoff: lender issues release of deed of trust, trustee issues reconveyance deed, promissory note marked \"paid\" and returned.\n\n**Mortgage (Lien Theory States):** Two parties: lender and borrower. Lender places a LIEN on the property; borrower holds legal title. May require JUDICIAL foreclosure (through courts) unless mortgage includes a power of sale clause. At payoff: lender issues satisfaction/release of mortgage, promissory note marked \"paid\" and returned.\n\n**Promissory Note:** Borrower's promise to repay. A negotiable instrument \u2014 can be transferred (sold) to another holder. Accompanied by a security instrument (deed of trust or mortgage) that pledges the property as collateral."},{heading:"B. Types of Loans",body:"**Conventional Loans:** Not government-insured or guaranteed. May require 20% down (80% LTV).\n- **Conforming:** Meets Fannie Mae/Freddie Mac loan limits and guidelines. Lenders can sell to Fannie/Freddie.\n- **Non-Conforming:** Doesn't meet Fannie/Freddie guidelines. Higher rates and/or PMI required.\n- **Jumbo Loan:** Exceeds conforming loan limits but meets other conventional criteria. Non-conforming.\n\n**FHA-Insured Loans:** Federal Housing Administration insures lenders against borrower default. Available to anyone who qualifies. Minimum 3.5% down payment. MIP (Mortgage Insurance Premium) required for LIFE of loan \u2014 upfront at closing + annual premium. FHA lending limits apply.\n\n**VA-Guaranteed Loans:** Department of Veterans Affairs guarantees loans for qualifying veterans. NO down payment, NO mortgage insurance, NO prepayment penalties. Eligibility: 181 days active duty or 3 months during wartime. VA guarantees up to 25% of the loan amount.\n\n**USDA/Rural Development Loans:** For family farms and rural housing. Up to 100% financing. Terms up to 33 years (38 for very low-income). USDA guarantees up to 95% of loss.\n\n**Amortization:**\n- **Fully Amortized:** Principal paid down over the full loan term; equal monthly payments with increasing principal portion and decreasing interest.\n- **Partially Amortized (Balloon):** Partial amortization during the term; large balloon payment at end to pay off remaining principal.\n- **Interest-Only:** Borrower pays only interest during the term; principal unchanged until end.\n\n**Adjustable-Rate Mortgage (ARM):** Interest rate fluctuates based on an economic index (e.g., LIBOR). Typically lower initial rate. **Negative amortization** occurs when a payment doesn't cover the interest due \u2014 the unpaid interest is ADDED to the loan principal, INCREASING the debt.\n\n**Owner Financing:**\n- **Land Contract (Contract for Deed):** Buyer makes installment payments to seller. Seller retains title; buyer gets equitable title.\n- **Purchase Money Mortgage:** Seller issues a loan to the buyer as part of the transaction.\n- **Wrap-Around Mortgage:** Seller's new mortgage wraps around their existing mortgage; seller continues paying first mortgage while buyer pays seller.\n\n**Reverse Mortgage:** Lender pays homeowner using home equity. For older homeowners wanting to stay in their homes.\n\n**Home Equity Loan / HELOC:** Loans based on home equity. HELOC allows borrowing as needed with interest-only payments typical."}],keyTerms:[]},{topicNumber:4,topicName:"Property Ownership",overview:"Property Ownership accounts for approximately 8% of the Maryland PSI exam. You must understand the distinction between real and personal property, the five fixture tests (MARIA), the bundle of rights, three legal description methods (metes and bounds, lot and block, RGSS), land measurement calculations (acre = 43,560 sq ft, mile = 5,280 ft), types of encumbrances (liens and easements), types of co-ownership (tenancy in common, joint tenancy with right of survivorship), common-interest ownership (condos, co-ops, timeshares, PUDs), freehold estates (fee simple, life estate, fee simple defeasible), and leasehold estates. Maryland-specific topics include ground rent (unique to MD), recordation and transfer taxes, and MD HOA/condo laws.",sections:[{heading:"A. Real vs. Personal Property \u2014 Fixtures and the Bundle of Rights",body:"**Real Property:** Land plus anything permanently attached to the land or to permanent structures on the land. Includes the surface, subsurface (mineral rights), and air rights.\n\n**Personal Property (Chattel):** Movable items not permanently attached to the land. Can be converted to real property through annexation.\n\n**Fixture:** An item that was once personal property but has been attached to real property in such a way that it is now considered part of the real estate. Fixtures are included in a sale unless negotiated otherwise.\n\n**Annexation:** The process of converting personal property to real property (attaching it).\n\n**Severance:** The process of removing a fixture, converting it back to personal property.\n\n**Five Fixture Tests (MARIA):** Courts use these tests to determine if an item is a fixture or personal property:\n- **M**ethod of Annexation \u2014 How is the item attached? Is the attachment permanent?\n- **A**daptability for Use \u2014 Was the item custom-fitted for the property? Would removal destroy its usefulness?\n- **R**elationship of the Parties \u2014 Courts favor tenants over landlords and buyers over sellers in disputes.\n- **I**ntention in Placing \u2014 Was the item intended to be temporary or permanent?\n- **A**greement of the Parties \u2014 If the parties agreed on what stays/goes, this is the simplest and first test.\n\n**Emblements (Fructus Industriales):** Cultivated crops that are considered PERSONAL property even though they grow in the soil. A farmer who has planted crops is entitled to harvest them even if the land is sold before harvest.\n\n**Bundle of Rights:** The rights that come with property ownership: Possession, Control, Exclusion, Enjoyment, and Disposition (sale/transfer). These rights can be separated \u2014 e.g., mineral rights sold separately, or property leased (denying the owner possession)."},{heading:"B. Land Characteristics and Legal Descriptions",body:"**Physical Characteristics of Land:**\n- **Immobility:** Geographic location is fixed; land cannot be moved.\n- **Indestructibility:** Land itself does not deteriorate (improvements do).\n- **Uniqueness (Nonhomogeneity):** No two parcels are exactly alike.\n\n### Three Legal Description Methods\n\n**1. Metes and Bounds:**\n- **Metes** = direction and distance of boundary lines\n- **Bounds** = physical features (monuments) that define boundaries\n- Begins and ends at a Point of Beginning (POB)\n- Uses monuments (trees, rocks, stakes, creeks) \u2014 monuments have MORE credibility than measurements in disputes\n- Oldest and least precise method\n\n**2. Lot and Block (Recorded Plat):**\n- References a recorded subdivision plat map\n- Example: \"Lot 6 of Block 3 of the East Subdivision plat as recorded in Map Book 18, Page 11\"\n- Includes streets, access roads, and utilities shown on plat\n- Most common method in urban/suburban areas\n\n**3. Rectangular Government Survey System (RGSS):**\n- Uses principal meridians (north-south lines) and base lines (east-west lines)\n- Township = 6 miles x 6 miles = 36 square miles\n- Section = 1 mile x 1 mile = 1 square mile = 640 acres\n- Each township contains 36 sections\n- Township lines run east-west; range lines run north-south\n- Descriptions read right to left: \"S 1/2 SE 1/4 NW 1/4 Section 14, Township Clarkson\"\n- 37 named principal meridians in the U.S.\n\n**Land Measurement Formulas (MEMORIZE):**\n- 1 acre = 43,560 square feet\n- 1 mile = 5,280 linear feet\n- 1 square mile = 640 acres\n- 1 hectare \u2248 2.47 acres\n- Square feet / 43,560 = acres\n- Acres x 43,560 = square feet\n\n**Measuring Structures:**\n- Area of rectangle = length x width\n- Area of triangle = 1/2 base x height\n- 1 square yard = 9 square feet\n- Ceiling height must be at least 7 feet to count as finished square footage\n- Garages are NOT included in finished square footage\n- Stairwells ARE included\n\n**Datum:** Horizontal reference point for measuring elevation.\n**Benchmark:** Point of known exact elevation, marked with a brass or aluminum plate."},{heading:"C. Encumbrances \u2014 Liens, Easements, and Restrictions",body:"An **encumbrance** is a legal limitation on the owner's use of the property or its value. It affects marketability and may be monetary or physical.\n\n### Liens (Financial Encumbrances)\n\n**General Liens** \u2014 affect ALL property (real and personal):\n- Judgment liens\n- Federal and state tax liens\n- Decedent's debts\n\n**Specific Liens** \u2014 affect a SPECIFIC property:\n- Mortgage liens\n- Property tax liens\n- Mechanic's liens\n- Special assessment liens\n- HOA liens\n- Vendor/vendee liens\n\n**Voluntary vs. Involuntary Liens:**\n- Voluntary: Mortgage lien (borrower chooses)\n- Involuntary: Property tax lien, mechanic's lien (imposed without consent)\n\n**Priority of Liens (typical order):**\n1. Property tax liens (ALWAYS first)\n2. Mechanic's liens (if work began before mortgage was recorded)\n3. First mortgage lien\n4. All other liens by date of recording\n\n**Mechanic's Lien:** Placed by a vendor of goods/services who was not paid for work on the property. Creates a cloud on title.\n\n**HOA Super Lien:** Many states (including Maryland) give HOA liens statutory super-lien status, taking priority over first mortgages for a limited amount (typically 6 months of dues).\n\n### Easements (Non-Possessory Use Rights)\n\nAn **easement** is a non-possessory right to use another's land for a specific purpose.\n- **Servient Estate:** The property burdened by the easement (\"suffers\" the easement)\n- **Dominant Estate:** The property or person benefiting from the easement (\"enjoys\" the easement)\n\n**Categories:**\n- **Easement Appurtenant:** Attached to a specific parcel; transfers with the land (\"runs with the land\")\n- **Easement in Gross:** Granted to a specific individual or business (e.g., utility easement); not tied to a dominant estate\n\n**Types:**\n- **Easement by Necessity:** Created for ingress/egress when land is landlocked\n- **Easement by Prescription:** Acquired through continued, uninterrupted, obvious, exclusive, and ADVERSE use without permission (like adverse possession but for use rights)\n- **Right-of-Way:** A pass-through right; benefits anyone needing passage\n\n**Easement Termination:** Express agreement, abandonment (dominant estate takes action showing clear intent to stop using), merger (one party buys the other property \u2014 you can't have an easement on your own land), or when no longer needed.\n\n**License:** TEMPORARY permission to use land; not assignable or inheritable; revocable at owner's discretion. Different from an easement (which is a permanent property interest).\n\n**Encroachments:** Structures or objects built on another's land without permission. Always illegal. A survey identifies encroachments.\n\n**Deed Restrictions / CC&Rs:** Private restrictions on land use that run with the land. Enforceable by HOA or neighbors."},{heading:"D. Water Rights and Natural Processes",body:"### Water Rights\n\n**Riparian Rights:** Rights to water that MOVES through a property (river, stream).\n- Navigable water: Owner owns to the water's edge; state owns the waterway\n- Non-navigable water: Owner owns to the center of the waterway\n\n**Littoral Rights:** Rights to STATIC water adjacent to a property (pond, lake, ocean). Owner has right to use and enjoy but NOT to divert or contain the water.\n\n**Doctrine of Prior Appropriation:** First party to take water from a source and put it to beneficial use maintains the claim. Common in western states with scarce water. \"First in time, first in right.\"\n\n### Natural Processes\n\n- **Accretion:** Gradual build-up of land by water depositing rock, sand, and soil\n- **Alluvion:** The new soil/land deposits created by accretion\n- **Erosion:** Gradual loss of land due to natural forces\n- **Avulsion:** SUDDEN loss of land by swift, large-scale change in water flow (e.g., flood)\n- **Reliction:** Gradual receding of water uncovering new land\n\n### Subsurface and Air Rights\n- **Mineral Rights (Subsurface):** Rights to drill/dig for minerals; can be sold separately from surface rights\n- **Air Rights:** Rights to use the space above a property; can be sold or leased separately"},{heading:"E. Types of Ownership \u2014 Freehold Estates",body:"A **freehold estate** is ownership of indefinite duration (vs. leasehold, which is for a fixed period).\n\n### Fee Simple Estate (Fee Simple Absolute)\nThe HIGHEST and most complete form of ownership. Gives the owner the full bundle of rights. Freely transferable, inheritable, and of indefinite duration. The owner holds surface, subsurface, and air rights (unless previously separated). Must go through probate before distribution to heirs.\n\n### Fee Simple Defeasible (Qualified Fee)\nOwnership subject to the occurrence or non-occurrence of a specific event. Two types:\n\n**Fee Simple Determinable:** Ownership lasts \"as long as\" a condition is met. AUTOMATICALLY reverts to the original owner (or their heirs) if the condition is violated. The original owner retains a **possibility of reverter**.\n- Example: \"To the church as long as it is used for religious purposes\" \u2014 if the church starts a business there, ownership AUTOMATICALLY reverts.\n\n**Fee Simple Subject to Condition Subsequent:** Ownership granted with a condition. If condition is violated, the original owner has the RIGHT to re-acquire (right of re-entry), but must go to COURT \u2014 not automatic.\n- Example: \"To Gabriel on condition the property remains residential\" \u2014 if Gabriel converts it, Trinity must sue to reclaim.\n\n### Life Estate\nOwnership limited to someone's lifetime. The life tenant must maintain the property and not commit **waste** (acts that significantly reduce the property's value).\n\n- **Ordinary Life Estate:** Based on the life of the estate holder. Ends when the life tenant dies.\n- **Life Estate Pur Autre Vie:** Based on the life of someone OTHER than the estate holder. \"For the life of Rose.\"\n\nWhen a life estate ends:\n- **Remainder:** Ownership passes to a **remainderman** (someone other than the grantor)\n- **Reversion:** Ownership returns to the original grantor (or their heirs)\n\n### Leasehold Estates (Non-Freehold)\nA possessory interest without ownership:\n- **Estate for Years:** Fixed term with a specific end date; no notice needed to terminate\n- **Periodic Estate:** Automatically renews (e.g., month-to-month); requires notice to terminate\n- **Estate at Will:** No fixed end date; either party can terminate at any time\n- **Estate at Sufferance:** Tenant stays after lease expires (holdover tenant); tenant is a trespasser"},{heading:"F. Types of Co-Ownership",body:"### Tenancy in Common (TIC)\n- Each co-owner has an undivided interest in the whole property\n- Ownership shares can be UNEQUAL\n- Each owner's interest is inheritable \u2014 passes to heirs, NOT to co-owners\n- Co-owner can sell their interest without other owners' consent\n- DEFAULT form of co-ownership if the deed doesn't specify\n- Termination: partition action (physical division or sale) or all parties selling\n\n### Joint Tenancy\n- Requires FOUR UNITIES: Time, Title, Interest, Possession (TTIP)\n- All owners acquired interest at the same TIME, from the same TITLE (deed), with equal INTEREST, and equal right of POSSESSION\n- Includes **Right of Survivorship** \u2014 when one joint tenant dies, their interest passes to surviving joint tenants automatically; NOT through will or probate\n- If a joint tenant sells their interest, the buyer becomes a TENANT IN COMMON with the remaining joint tenants (breaks the unities of time and title)\n- Two joint tenants \u2192 death of one \u2192 survivor owns in severalty\n- Three+ joint tenants \u2192 shares pass equally to survivors\n\n### Tenancy by the Entirety\n- Available only to MARRIED couples\n- Includes right of survivorship\n- Neither spouse can sell or encumber without the other's consent\n- Protected from individual creditors of one spouse\n- Recognized in Maryland and some other states\n\n### Ownership in Severalty\n- One person owns the property alone; no co-owners\n- Upon death, passes by will or intestate succession\n\n### Partition\n- Legal action to divide co-owned property when co-owners disagree\n- Physical partition (divide the land) or sale partition (sell and split proceeds)"},{heading:"G. Common-Interest Ownership \u2014 Condos, Co-ops, Timeshares, PUDs",body:"### Condominium\n- Owner owns individual UNIT (fee simple) plus undivided shared interest in common areas\n- Each unit has its own legal description and tax bill\n- HOA/Condo association maintains common areas and exterior; owners pay HOA fees\n- Owner does NOT own the land or building structure individually\n\n### Cooperative (Co-op)\n- A CORPORATION owns the building and land\n- Residents own SHARES in the corporation, NOT real estate\n- Right to occupy comes from a PROPRIETARY LEASE\n- One property tax bill for the entire building (corporation pays)\n- Board typically must approve buyers\n\n### Townhouse\n- Single-family attached home; owner owns the structure AND the land beneath it\n- May share walls with neighbors (party walls)\n- May have HOA for common areas\n\n### Planned Unit Development (PUD)\n- Mixed-use development (residential + commercial)\n- Owner owns individual parcel plus shared common areas\n- HOA maintains common areas\n- Developers must typically submit plat maps\n\n### Timeshare\n- Multiple owners share rights to use a property, typically in weekly intervals\n- **Timeshare Estate (Fee Simple):** Owner owns a fractional interest in the property; can be sold or inherited\n- **Timeshare Use (Right-to-Use):** Only the right to occupy for a specific time; NOT ownership; cannot be inherited\n- 52 weeks in a year; a 1/13 interest = 4 weeks (52 / 13 = 4)\n\n### Campground Membership\n- Right to use developer's facilities; may use campground any time it's open\n- NOT a real property interest"},{heading:"Maryland Supplement \u2014 Property Ownership",body:"**Maryland Ground Rent:** A unique MD ownership form where a homeowner owns the improvements but leases the land under a long-term ground lease (typically 99 years, renewable). The ground rent holder owns the land. Common in Baltimore City. Maryland law gives ground rent tenants the right to redeem (buy out) the ground rent.\n\n**Maryland Recordation Tax:** MD imposes a recordation tax on deeds and security instruments recorded in land records. Rate varies by county \u2014 typically $3.50 to $5.00 per $500 of consideration for deeds. Different rates apply to mortgages/security instruments.\n\n**Maryland Transfer Tax:** State transfer tax is 0.5% of consideration. Many counties add a local transfer tax (typically 0.5% to 2%). Total combined state and local transfer taxes common in MD real estate closings.\n\n**Maryland Condominium Act:** Governs condo creation, sales, and operations. Requires public offering statement for new condo sales. Gives buyers a right to cancel within 15 days of receiving the offering statement.\n\n**Maryland Homeowners Association Act:** Regulates HOAs; requires disclosure of HOA documents to buyers; limits what HOAs can restrict.\n\n**Maryland HOA Super Lien:** MD law gives HOA liens priority over first mortgages for up to 6 months of regular assessments (not fines or late fees).\n\n**Maryland Tenancy by the Entirety:** Recognized in Maryland for married couples. Neither spouse can unilaterally sell, convey, or encumber the property. Protects the property from individual creditors of one spouse."}],keyTerms:[]},{topicNumber:5,topicName:"Valuation",overview:"Valuation accounts for approximately 8% of the Maryland PSI exam. You must understand the difference between an appraisal (formal opinion of value by a licensed appraiser under USPAP) and a CMA (informal market estimate by a licensee). Know the three approaches to value \u2014 Sales Comparison (residential, based on substitution), Cost (unique/non-income properties, land value + depreciated improvement cost), and Income (rental/investment properties, using GRM, GIM, or cap rate). Master the four characteristics of value (DUST), economic principles (substitution, conformity, contribution, plottage, regression, progression, anticipation), types of depreciation (physical, functional, external), and the reconciliation process. Calculation questions on GRM, cap rate, and square footage adjustments are commonly tested.",sections:[{heading:"A. Appraisals \u2014 USPAP, FIRREA, and Appraiser Independence",body:"**Appraisal:** A formal, supportable opinion of value by a licensed or certified appraiser, performed for a specific purpose, party, and property, as of a specific date, and in accordance with USPAP (Uniform Standards of Professional Appraisal Practice).\n\n**Real estate licensees CANNOT perform appraisals.** Only licensed or certified appraisers may perform appraisals. Licensees perform CMAs (Comparative Market Analyses).\n\n**FIRREA (Financial Institutions Reform, Recovery, and Enforcement Act of 1989):** Requires that appraisals for federally related transactions (loans involving federal financial institutions) must be completed by state-certified or licensed appraisers.\n\n**FHA Loans:** Appraisal must be by a state-licensed appraiser approved by HUD.\n**VA Loans:** Appraisal must be by a VA-certified, state-licensed appraiser.\n\n**Appraiser Independence Requirements (AIR):** Implemented in 2010 by Fannie Mae, Freddie Mac, and FHFA. Key provisions:\n- Appraisers must be certified/licensed in the state where the property is located\n- Appraisers must be knowledgeable about the local market\n- Lenders are PROHIBITED from attempting to influence or coerce an appraiser's value opinion\n- Lenders selling to Fannie Mae must comply with AIR\n\n**USPAP Appraisal Steps:**\n1. State the problem (identify property, rights, purpose, type of value)\n2. Identify data needed\n3. Gather and analyze data (general area + property-specific)\n4. Determine highest and best use\n5. Estimate land value (as if vacant)\n6. Apply one or more of the three approaches to value\n7. Reconcile (correlate) values \u2014 NOT an average; weight given to the most reliable approach\n8. Develop and deliver the appraisal report"},{heading:"B. Value Concepts \u2014 Market Value, DUST, and Economic Principles",body:"**Market Value:** The most probable price a property will sell for in an open market, with neither buyer nor seller under duress, with reasonable exposure time.\n\n**Market Price:** What a buyer actually paid and a seller accepted \u2014 may differ from market value.\n\n**Cost:** The amount to recreate the property (reproduction or replacement).\n\n**Value:** A property's objective worth; may not equal price or cost.\n\n### DUST \u2014 Four Characteristics of Value\n- **D**emand \u2014 How popular/desirable is the property?\n- **U**tility \u2014 The property's function and usefulness\n- **S**carcity \u2014 Limited supply relative to demand\n- **T**ransferability \u2014 Ease with which title can be transferred; title defects reduce value\n\n### Other Types of Value\n- **Value in Use:** What the property is worth to the person using it (subjective)\n- **Assessed Value:** What the taxing authority thinks it's worth (for property tax)\n- **Mortgage Value:** What it can be loaned on or sold for at foreclosure\n- **Insured Value:** Replacement/rebuild cost for insurance purposes\n- **Investment Value:** Return on investment the property may generate\n\n### Economic Principles of Value\n- **Substitution:** A buyer won't pay more for a property than the cost of a comparable substitute. This is the basis of the sales comparison approach.\n- **Conformity:** Value is highest when properties conform to surrounding neighborhood standards.\n- **Contribution:** The value of an improvement is measured by what it adds to the overall property value, not its cost.\n- **Plottage:** Increase in value from combining adjacent parcels into a single larger parcel.\n- **Assemblage:** The PROCESS of combining adjacent parcels (plottage is the increase in value from doing so).\n- **Regression:** A lower-value property benefits from being near higher-value properties; a higher-value property loses value near lower-value ones.\n- **Progression:** Increase in value from being near higher-value properties.\n- **Anticipation:** Value changes based on expectation of future events (e.g., a proposed transit station increases nearby values before it's built).\n- **Competition:** Value is influenced by what else is available in the market.\n- **Highest and Best Use:** The legal and feasible use that would be most profitable \u2014 a fundamental appraisal concept."},{heading:"C. Three Approaches to Value",body:"### 1. Sales Comparison Approach\n- Based on the principle of SUBSTITUTION\n- Most commonly used for single-family homes and residential properties\n- Compares the subject property to recently sold similar properties (comparables/comps)\n- Appraiser adjusts the COMPARABLE'S sales price (not the subject's) for differences\n- Adjustments in this order: financing terms, conditions of sale, market conditions, location, physical characteristics\n- **Bracketing:** Using both superior and inferior comps to determine a probable value range\n- Fewer adjustments needed = more objective the appraisal\n- Comps should be arm's length transactions (unrelated parties, no duress)\n\n### 2. Cost Approach\n- Based on: Land value (as if vacant) + Depreciated cost of improvements = Market value\n- Used for UNIQUE properties that aren't income-producing: churches, schools, hospitals, theaters, newly constructed homes\n- Two cost methods:\n  - **Replacement Cost:** Cost to build a functionally equivalent property using modern materials/methods\n  - **Reproduction Cost:** Cost to build an EXACT replica with same materials and deficiencies\n- Depreciation is subtracted because the existing structure is not new\n- Three causes of depreciation (covered in Section D)\n\n### 3. Income Approach\n- Most reliable for income-producing properties: apartments, shopping centers, office buildings\n- Three methods:\n\n**Gross Rent Multiplier (GRM):** For 1-4 unit residential properties\n- GRM = Sales Price / Gross Monthly Rent\n- Value = Gross Monthly Rent x GRM\n\n**Gross Income Multiplier (GIM):** For 5+ unit commercial properties\n- GIM = Sales Price / Gross Annual Income\n- Value = Gross Annual Income x GIM\n\n**Capitalization Rate (Cap Rate):** For all income properties\n- Cap Rate (R) = Net Operating Income (I) / Value (V)\n- Value (V) = NOI (I) / Cap Rate (R)\n- NOI (I) = Cap Rate (R) x Value (V)\n\n**Calculating NOI:**\n1. Potential Gross Income (if fully rented all year)\n2. - Vacancy and collection loss\n3. = Effective Gross Income\n4. - Operating expenses (NOT including debt service/mortgage payments)\n5. = Net Operating Income (NOI)"},{heading:"D. Depreciation in Appraisals",body:"**Depreciation** in appraisal is LOSS IN VALUE \u2014 unrelated to tax depreciation (which is about cost recovery).\n\n### Three Causes of Depreciation\n\n**1. Physical Deterioration:** Wear and tear, damage, improper maintenance, aging. The most common and visible form.\n\n**2. Functional Obsolescence:** Loss in value from defects in design or outdated features. Examples: 4-bedroom home with only 1 bathroom; outdated electrical system; overbuilt home for the neighborhood (superadequacy).\n\n**3. External Depreciation (Economic Obsolescence):** Loss in value from factors OUTSIDE the property \u2014 incurable by the owner. Examples: nearby airport noise, declining neighborhood, rezoning of adjacent land, increased traffic.\n\n### Curable vs. Incurable\n- **Curable:** The cost to fix is LESS THAN or EQUAL TO the increase in value. Worth fixing.\n- **Incurable:** The cost to fix EXCEEDS the increase in value. Not practical to correct.\n\nBoth physical deterioration and functional obsolescence can be curable or incurable. External depreciation is ALWAYS incurable (the owner can't fix the neighborhood)."},{heading:"E. Comparative Market Analysis (CMA)",body:"A **CMA** is an INFORMAL estimate of market value performed by a real estate licensee (NOT an appraiser). Used to help sellers set listing prices and buyers decide offers.\n\n### CMA Best Practices\n- Use at least 3 comparable properties (comps)\n- Comps should be **arm's length transactions** \u2014 unrelated parties, no duress\n- Foreclosures, short sales, and distressed sales should NOT be used as comps (unless pricing for similar conditions)\n- Account for seller concessions (closing costs paid by seller, special financing)\n- Recent sales (3-6 months) carry MORE weight than older sales\n- Adjust the COMPARABLE'S price, not the subject property's price\n- If comparable is INFERIOR to subject \u2192 adjust UPWARD\n- If comparable is SUPERIOR to subject \u2192 adjust DOWNWARD\n\n### Additional Market Data\n- **Active Listings:** Show current competition; not true comps (no final sale price)\n- **Pending Listings:** Under contract; give insight on days on market (DOM)\n- **Expired Listings:** Listed but didn't sell; can indicate overpricing\n\n### CMA vs. Appraisal\n| Feature | CMA | Appraisal |\n|---------|-----|-----------|\n| Performed by | Real estate licensee | Licensed/certified appraiser |\n| Purpose | Help clients with pricing decisions | Formal opinion of value for lending |\n| Standards | No legal standards | USPAP |\n| Cost | Usually free | Paid by buyer/borrower |\n| Used for | Listing price, offer price | Mortgage lending, tax assessment |"},{heading:"F. Appraisal Math \u2014 GRM, Cap Rate, and Adjustments",body:"### GRM (Gross Rent Multiplier) \u2014 for 1-4 unit residential\n- GRM = Sales Price / Gross Monthly Rent\n- Value = Gross Monthly Rent x GRM\n\nExample: A duplex sold for $360,000. Monthly rent is $3,000. GRM = $360,000 / $3,000 = 120.\n\nExample: A property rents for $2,500/month. Area GRM is 140. Value = $2,500 x 140 = $350,000.\n\n### Cap Rate \u2014 for income-producing properties\n- I (NOI) / R (Cap Rate) = V (Value)\n- I / V = R\n- R x V = I\n\nExample: A property has NOI of $60,000 and sold for $750,000. Cap rate = $60,000 / $750,000 = 0.08 = 8%.\n\nExample: NOI is $45,000, market cap rate is 9%. Value = $45,000 / 0.09 = $500,000.\n\n### Square Footage Adjustments\n- Find the price per square foot of the comparable\n- Multiply by the difference in square footage\n- Add or subtract from the comparable's price\n\nExample: Comp sold for $300,000 and is 2,000 sq ft ($150/sq ft). Subject is 2,200 sq ft (200 sq ft more). Adjustment: $150 x 200 = +$30,000. Adjusted comp value = $330,000."},{heading:"Maryland Supplement \u2014 Valuation",body:"**Maryland Appraiser Licensing:** Maryland requires appraisers to be licensed or certified by the Maryland Commission of Real Estate Appraisers, Appraisal Management Companies, and Home Inspectors. Three license levels: Licensed Appraiser, Certified Residential Appraiser, and Certified General Appraiser. All must complete qualifying education, experience hours, and pass a national exam.\n\n**Maryland Property Tax Assessment:** Maryland assesses property at 100% of market value (fair market value). Reassessments occur every 3 years on a county rotation. Homeowners may appeal their assessment to the Property Tax Assessment Appeal Board.\n\n**Maryland Homestead Property Tax Credit:** Caps annual assessment increases at a percentage set by each county (typically 10% or less for the state portion). Protects homeowners from rapid tax increases due to rising property values.\n\n**Transfer and Recordation Tax Basis:** Maryland transfer and recordation taxes are calculated on the consideration (purchase price), making accurate property valuation critical at closing."}],keyTerms:[]},{topicNumber:6,topicName:"Land Use Controls",overview:"Land Use Controls covers public and private restrictions on property use. Know the four government powers (PETE): Police Power, Eminent Domain, Taxation, Escheat. Understand zoning types, variances, nonconforming uses, building codes, comprehensive plans, subdivision regulations, private deed restrictions and CC&Rs, and environmental regulations (CERCLA, lead-based paint, asbestos, radon, mold, underground storage tanks). Maryland-specific: critical area protections under the Chesapeake Bay Critical Area Act, and local county zoning authorities.",sections:[{heading:"A. Government Powers (PETE)",body:"Four government powers affect real property ownership:\n\n**1. Police Power:** Government's right to regulate land use to protect public health, safety, morals, and general welfare. Exercised through zoning, building codes, subdivision regulations, and environmental laws. No compensation is required because it's regulation, not taking.\n\n**2. Eminent Domain:** Government's right to take private property for PUBLIC USE with JUST COMPENSATION (fair market value). The taking process is called CONDEMNATION. The 5th Amendment requires just compensation. If the government takes only part of a property, the owner is compensated for the part taken plus any severance damages to the remainder.\n\n**Inverse Condemnation:** When the government effectively takes property (e.g., by regulation making it unusable) without formal eminent domain proceedings \u2014 the owner sues the government for compensation.\n\n**3. Taxation:** Government's power to levy property taxes to fund public services. Properties are assessed, and tax rates (mill rates) are applied. Unpaid taxes result in a tax lien, which always has first priority.\n\n**4. Escheat:** When a property owner dies without a will and no heirs can be found, the property reverts to the STATE. This prevents property from being ownerless."},{heading:"B. Zoning",body:"**Zoning:** The primary tool of police power. Local governments divide land into zones (residential, commercial, industrial, agricultural, mixed-use) and regulate what can be built and used in each.\n\n**Zoning Ordinances Cover:**\n- Use (residential, commercial, industrial)\n- Density (lot size, building height, setbacks)\n- Parking requirements\n- Signage\n- Buffer zones between incompatible uses\n\n**Comprehensive Plan (Master Plan):** Long-term land use plan adopted by local government. Zoning must be consistent with the comprehensive plan.\n\n**Variance:** Permission to deviate from zoning requirements for a specific property due to UNIQUE HARDSHIP (not self-created). Two types:\n- **Area Variance:** Relief from dimensional requirements (setbacks, height, lot size)\n- **Use Variance:** Permission for a use not allowed in the zone (harder to obtain)\n\n**Conditional/Special Use Permit:** Permission for a use that is allowed in the zone but requires special review due to potential impacts (e.g., school in residential zone, daycare).\n\n**Nonconforming Use:** A use that was legal when established but no longer complies with current zoning. Generally allowed to CONTINUE (grandfathered) but cannot be EXPANDED. If destroyed beyond a certain percentage, may lose grandfathered status.\n\n**Spot Zoning:** Rezoning a single parcel for a different use than surrounding properties \u2014 generally illegal unless justified by public interest.\n\n**Zoning Board of Appeals:** Hears variance requests, special permits, and appeals of zoning decisions."},{heading:"C. Private Land Use Controls",body:"**Deed Restrictions (Restrictive Covenants):** Private limitations on land use placed in the deed by a previous owner. They RUN WITH THE LAND \u2014 binding on all future owners. Examples: minimum square footage, architectural requirements, no commercial use.\n\n**CC&Rs (Covenants, Conditions, and Restrictions):** Comprehensive private restrictions typically found in subdivisions and planned communities. Enforced by the HOA or neighboring property owners through legal action.\n\n**Enforcement:** Private restrictions are enforced through injunctions (court orders to stop violations) or lawsuits for damages. Government does NOT enforce private restrictions.\n\n**Expiration:** Deed restrictions may have expiration dates or may be terminated by mutual agreement of affected property owners. Some old restrictions (e.g., racial covenants) are illegal and unenforceable.\n\n**Deed Restrictions vs. Zoning:** Deed restrictions are PRIVATE (neighbors/HOA enforce) and can be MORE restrictive than zoning but not less. Zoning is PUBLIC (government enforces)."},{heading:"D. Environmental Regulations",body:"**CERCLA (Comprehensive Environmental Response, Compensation, and Liability Act / Superfund):** Federal law imposing strict, joint, and several liability on property owners for hazardous waste cleanup \u2014 even if they didn't cause the contamination. Current owners can be liable. This is why environmental assessments (Phase I, Phase II) are critical in commercial real estate.\n\n**Lead-Based Paint:** Federal law requires disclosure of known lead-based paint in homes built before 1978. Buyers must receive the EPA pamphlet \"Protect Your Family From Lead in Your Home\" and have a 10-day opportunity to conduct a risk assessment.\n\n**Asbestos:** Hazardous material common in older buildings (insulation, floor tiles, ceiling texture). Disclosure required in some states. Abatement requires licensed professionals.\n\n**Radon:** Naturally occurring radioactive gas that can accumulate in homes. No federal disclosure requirement, but many states require disclosure. Testing is recommended.\n\n**Mold:** Water damage and moisture can lead to mold growth. Some states require disclosure of known mold issues.\n\n**Underground Storage Tanks (USTs):** Leaking USTs are a common source of soil and groundwater contamination. Environmental assessments check for USTs."},{heading:"Maryland Supplement \u2014 Land Use",body:"**Chesapeake Bay Critical Area Act:** Maryland law protecting land within 1,000 feet of the Chesapeake Bay and its tributaries. Imposes strict development limits, buffer requirements, and impervious surface caps. Affects property use and development potential in many Maryland counties.\n\n**Maryland County Zoning:** Maryland is a strong home-rule state. Each county and Baltimore City has its own zoning code, comprehensive plan, and zoning board. There is NO statewide zoning.\n\n**Maryland Department of the Environment (MDE):** Enforces state environmental laws including lead paint abatement, asbestos, USTs, and water quality.\n\n**Maryland Lead Paint Registry:** Maryland maintains a lead paint registry and requires rental properties built before 1978 to be registered and inspected. This is stricter than federal requirements."}],keyTerms:[]},{topicNumber:7,topicName:"Transfer of Title",overview:"Transfer of Title covers how real property ownership is conveyed. Know the essential elements of a valid deed (grantor, grantee, consideration, granting clause, legal description, signature), types of deeds (general warranty, special warranty, bargain and sale, quitclaim), the difference between actual and constructive notice, recording acts (race, notice, race-notice), title insurance (owner's vs. lender's), the Torrens system, will and intestate succession basics, and adverse possession requirements (ONACHO). Maryland-specific: Maryland recording requirements, recordation tax, intestate succession rules, and the Maryland Torrens system (limited use).",sections:[{heading:"A. Essential Elements of a Valid Deed",body:"A deed is a written document that CONVEYS title to real property. Requirements for a valid deed:\n\n1. **Grantor** (seller) \u2014 must be legally competent and named\n2. **Grantee** (buyer) \u2014 must be identifiable; need not be competent\n3. **Consideration** \u2014 something of value exchanged (even nominal, like $1)\n4. **Granting Clause (Words of Conveyance)** \u2014 \"grants, bargains, sells, and conveys\"\n5. **Legal Description** \u2014 sufficient to identify the property\n6. **Signature of Grantor** \u2014 must be signed by the grantor (seller)\n7. **Delivery and Acceptance** \u2014 deed must be delivered to and accepted by the grantee. TITLE TRANSFERS upon DELIVERY AND ACCEPTANCE, NOT upon recording.\n\nRecording a deed provides CONSTRUCTIVE NOTICE to the world but is NOT required for a valid transfer between grantor and grantee. However, recording protects the grantee against subsequent claims.\n\n**Acknowledgment:** The grantor's signature is acknowledged before a notary public. Required for recording but NOT for validity between the parties."},{heading:"B. Types of Deeds",body:"**General Warranty Deed:** The BEST deed for the buyer. Grantor warrants title against ALL claims, including those from prior owners. Contains FIVE covenants (warranties):\n1. Seisin \u2014 grantor owns and has right to convey\n2. Quiet Enjoyment \u2014 grantee won't be evicted by superior claim\n3. Against Encumbrances \u2014 no undisclosed liens/encumbrances\n4. Further Assurance \u2014 grantor will do what's needed to perfect title\n5. Warranty Forever \u2014 grantor will defend against all claims\n\nThe first three are PRESENT covenants (breach occurs at delivery if false). The last two are FUTURE covenants (breach only if problem arises later).\n\n**Special Warranty Deed:** Grantor warrants against claims arising ONLY during their ownership period \u2014 not against claims from prior owners. Common in commercial transactions, trust/estate sales, and foreclosures.\n\n**Bargain and Sale Deed:** Implies grantor owns the property but provides NO warranties. Used in tax sales and some foreclosures.\n\n**Quitclaim Deed:** Conveys whatever interest the grantor MAY have \u2014 with NO warranties at all. \"I give you whatever I own, if anything.\" Used for clearing title defects, divorce settlements, and transfers between family members.\n\n**Deed of Trust:** Used in title theory states to convey legal title to a trustee as security for a loan. NOT a conveyance of full ownership.\n\n**Sheriff's/Referee's Deed:** Issued after a court-ordered foreclosure or execution sale. Carries no warranties."},{heading:"C. Title, Notice, and Recording",body:"**Title:** The right of ownership \u2014 the bundle of rights. Not a physical document (the DEED is the document that conveys title).\n\n**Actual Notice:** Direct knowledge \u2014 you saw it, heard it, or were told.\n\n**Constructive Notice:** Legal presumption of knowledge because information was RECORDED in public records. Everyone is presumed to know what's in the public record.\n\n**Chain of Title:** The recorded history of ownership transfers for a property. A title search examines the chain of title for defects.\n\n**Recording Acts:** State laws governing priority when there are conflicting claims. Three types:\n- **Race Statute:** First to RECORD wins (pure race to the courthouse)\n- **Notice Statute:** Later purchaser who paid value WITHOUT notice of prior claim wins\n- **Race-Notice Statute:** Later purchaser wins only if they paid value, had NO notice, AND recorded first\n\n**Marketable Title Act:** Extinguishes old claims and interests that are not re-recorded within a specified period (typically 30-40 years).\n\n**Title Abstract:** Summary of all recorded documents affecting title.\n\n**Attorney's Opinion of Title:** Review of the abstract by an attorney stating whether title is marketable.\n\n**Torrens System:** A system of title REGISTRATION (vs. recording) where the government certifies ownership. Used in limited form in a few states, including Maryland for certain properties."},{heading:"D. Title Insurance",body:"**Title Insurance:** Protects against LOSS from title defects that existed before the policy date. Unlike other insurance (which covers future risks), title insurance covers PAST events.\n\n**Owner's Title Insurance:** Protects the BUYER/OWNER for the purchase price. Optional but recommended. One-time premium at closing. Lasts as long as the owner or their heirs own the property.\n\n**Lender's Title Insurance:** Protects the LENDER up to the loan amount. Required by virtually all mortgage lenders. Borrower typically pays the premium. Coverage decreases as the loan balance decreases.\n\n**What Title Insurance Covers:** Forgery, undisclosed heirs, errors in public records, missing signatures, incorrect legal descriptions, undisclosed easements.\n\n**What Title Insurance Does NOT Cover:** Defects arising AFTER the policy date (new liens, new easements), zoning violations, environmental contamination, matters disclosed in the policy's exceptions."},{heading:"E. Wills, Intestate Succession, and Adverse Possession",body:"**Testate:** Dying with a valid will. Property passes according to the will's terms after probate.\n\n**Intestate:** Dying without a will. Property passes according to STATE intestate succession laws (typically: spouse first, then children, then parents, then siblings).\n\n**Probate:** Court process to validate a will, pay debts, and distribute assets. Fee simple estates must go through probate. Joint tenancy with right of survivorship and trust-owned property bypass probate.\n\n**Adverse Possession:** Acquiring title to another's land through open, notorious, actual, continuous, hostile, and exclusive possession for the statutory period (varies by state, typically 10-20 years; Maryland = 20 years). The adverse possessor's use must be without the true owner's permission.\n\n**Tacking:** Successive adverse possessors can ADD (tack) their periods of possession together to meet the statutory period, if they are in privity (successive relationship, not strangers).\n\n**Color of Title:** A document that appears to give title but is defective. An adverse possessor claiming under color of title may have advantages (shorter statutory period, more land claimed)."},{heading:"Maryland Supplement \u2014 Transfer of Title",body:"**Maryland Recording Requirements:** Deeds must be recorded in the county where the property is located. Maryland requires acknowledgment before a notary. Recordation tax applies.\n\n**Maryland Recordation Tax:** Imposed on deeds and security instruments ($3.50-$5.00 per $500 of consideration, varies by county). Based on the consideration amount stated in the deed.\n\n**Maryland Intestate Succession:** If decedent dies without a will in Maryland: spouse gets entire estate if no surviving children or parents. If decedent has surviving children: spouse gets 1/2, children share 1/2. If no spouse: children inherit equally, then parents, then siblings.\n\n**Maryland Adverse Possession:** Requires 20 years of actual, open, notorious, exclusive, hostile, and continuous possession. Maryland courts require clear and convincing evidence.\n\n**Maryland Torrens System:** Maryland has a limited Torrens title registration system in certain counties, but the vast majority of properties use the recording system."}],keyTerms:[]},{topicNumber:8,topicName:"Property Disclosures",overview:"Property Disclosures covers what sellers and agents must disclose to buyers. Know federal lead-based paint disclosure rules (pre-1978 homes, EPA pamphlet, 10-day assessment period), state-required seller disclosure forms, material fact disclosure duties (latent vs. patent defects), stigmatized property rules (psychological defects like death, crime, haunted houses), Megan's Law, flood zone disclosure, and the agent's duty to disclose known material facts. Maryland-specific: Maryland Residential Property Disclosure and Disclaimer Statement, Maryland latent defect disclosure requirements, and Maryland's approach to stigmatized properties (no duty to disclose psychological stigmas).",sections:[{heading:"A. Material Facts and Agent Disclosure Duties",body:"**Material Fact:** Any fact that would affect a reasonable buyer's decision to purchase or the price they'd pay. Agents have a duty to disclose ALL known material facts about a property \u2014 even if the seller doesn't want them disclosed.\n\n**Latent Defect:** A hidden defect that would NOT be discovered by ordinary inspection. MUST be disclosed. Examples: leaking basement behind finished walls, structural cracks covered by fresh paint, termite damage in inaccessible areas.\n\n**Patent Defect:** A defect that IS visible or discoverable by ordinary inspection. Generally no duty to disclose because the buyer can see it. Example: cracked window, worn carpet, peeling paint.\n\n**Agent's Duty:** Agents must disclose known material facts about the property, even if it harms the seller's interests. An agent who fails to disclose known material defects may be liable for misrepresentation or fraud.\n\n**Seller's Duty:** Sellers must disclose known material defects. Most states (including Maryland) require a written seller disclosure form. \"I didn't know\" is not a defense if the seller should have known (constructive knowledge).\n\n**Stigmatized Property:** A property with psychological or perceived defects \u2014 murder, suicide, haunting, former meth lab, neighboring sex offender. Disclosure rules vary by state. In Maryland, sellers/agents generally have NO duty to disclose psychological stigmas unless the buyer specifically asks."},{heading:"B. Federal Lead-Based Paint Disclosure",body:"**Applicability:** ALL residential properties built BEFORE 1978 (the year lead paint was banned for residential use). Applies to sales AND leases. Does NOT apply to:\n- Housing built in 1978 or later\n- 0-bedroom dwellings (studios/efficiencies)\n- Leases of less than 100 days\n- Housing certified lead-free by a certified inspector\n- Foreclosure sales\n\n**Required Disclosures:**\n1. EPA pamphlet \"Protect Your Family From Lead in Your Home\"\n2. Any known lead-based paint hazards on the property\n3. All available records and reports concerning lead-based paint\n\n**Buyer's Rights:**\n- 10-day period to conduct a risk assessment or inspection for lead-based paint (can be waived in writing)\n- The 10-day period may be shortened or extended by mutual agreement\n\n**Agent's Responsibility:** Ensure the seller complies. Agents are NOT required to inspect for lead paint but must ensure the disclosure form is signed and the pamphlet is provided.\n\n**Penalties:** Failure to comply can result in civil penalties up to $19,000+ per violation, plus treble (triple) damages, and the buyer may have the right to rescind the contract."},{heading:"C. Other Federal and State Disclosures",body:"**Flood Zone Disclosure:** If the property is in a Special Flood Hazard Area (SFHA) requiring flood insurance, this must be disclosed. FEMA flood maps determine flood zones.\n\n**Megan's Law:** Federal law requiring states to maintain sex offender registries and make them publicly available. Real estate agents typically have no duty to search or disclose sex offender information, but if asked, should direct buyers to the state registry. Some states require specific notices.\n\n**Asbestos:** No federal disclosure requirement for residential real estate, but some states require it. If known, it's a material fact and should be disclosed.\n\n**Radon:** No federal disclosure requirement. Many states require radon disclosure forms. Testing is recommended in high-radon areas.\n\n**Mold:** No federal disclosure requirement. Some states (not Maryland currently) require specific mold disclosure. Known mold is a material fact.\n\n**Death on Property:** Disclosure rules vary. Most states (including Maryland) do NOT require disclosure of death, suicide, or murder on a property \u2014 these are psychological stigmas, not physical defects.\n\n**HOA Disclosures:** Sellers must provide HOA documents (CC&Rs, bylaws, budget, reserve study) to buyers, typically within a specified period after contract. Buyers often have a review/right to cancel period."},{heading:"D. Fraud, Misrepresentation, and Puffing",body:"**Fraud (Intentional Misrepresentation):** Knowingly making a false statement of material fact with intent to induce reliance, where the other party relies on it and is harmed. Requires: false statement, knowledge of falsity, intent, reliance, damages.\n\n**Negligent Misrepresentation:** Making a false statement carelessly without verifying its truth. Agent says \"the roof is new\" without checking \u2014 if it's 15 years old, this is negligent misrepresentation.\n\n**Puffing (Puffery):** Exaggerated opinions that a reasonable person would not rely on as fact. \"This is the best house in the neighborhood\" = puffing. \"This house has a new roof\" = a factual statement, not puffing. The line matters.\n\n**Active Fraud (Concealment):** Actively hiding a defect \u2014 painting over water stains, moving furniture to cover floor damage. This IS illegal.\n\n**Silence as Misrepresentation:** Generally, silence is not fraud. However, silence about a KNOWN LATENT DEFECT can constitute misrepresentation if the defect is material and not discoverable.\n\n**Stigmatized Property Rule (MD):** Maryland law provides that psychological stigmas (death, suicide, homicide, felony, HIV status of occupant) do not need to be voluntarily disclosed. However, if the buyer directly asks, the agent should answer truthfully or advise the buyer that the seller refuses to answer."},{heading:"Maryland Supplement \u2014 Property Disclosures",body:"**Maryland Residential Property Disclosure and Disclaimer Statement:** Maryland requires sellers of residential properties (1-4 units) to complete and deliver a disclosure form to the buyer. The form covers: structural condition, roof, foundation, basement water, termites/wood-destroying insects, plumbing, electrical, HVAC, hazardous materials, and other known defects. The seller must disclose known defects but is NOT required to conduct an independent inspection.\n\n**Maryland Disclaimer Option:** Instead of completing the full disclosure, the seller may opt for a DISCLAIMER statement, selling the property \"as is\" with NO representations about condition. However, the seller may pay a fee/credit to the buyer (typically 0.5% of the sale price or a flat fee) for this option. The seller must still disclose known LATENT DEFECTS even with a disclaimer.\n\n**Maryland Latent Defect Rule:** Maryland follows the common law rule that sellers must disclose known latent material defects that would not be discoverable by reasonable inspection. A seller who knowingly conceals a latent defect can be sued for fraud.\n\n**Maryland Stigmatized Property Law:** Maryland law (Md. Code, Real Property \xa7 2-120) provides that a seller or agent is NOT required to disclose that a property was the site of a suicide, homicide, felony, or that an occupant had HIV/AIDS. These are not considered material facts. However, if a buyer asks, an agent should answer truthfully or state the seller declines to answer.\n\n**Maryland HOA/ Condo Disclosures:** Maryland Condominium Act requires delivery of public offering statement for new condo sales and resale disclosure packet for existing units. Buyer has review and cancellation rights."}],keyTerms:[]},{topicNumber:9,topicName:"Property Management",overview:"Property Management covers the role of property managers, types of leases, landlord-tenant law basics, the landlord's duties (habitability, quiet enjoyment, security deposits), the tenant's duties (pay rent, maintain property, no waste), eviction processes, fair housing compliance in rental management, the ADA and service animals, rent control, and security deposit rules. Maryland-specific: Maryland security deposit law (45-day return, interest requirements, itemized deductions), Maryland landlord-tenant code, and Maryland eviction procedures.",sections:[{heading:"A. Property Manager Role and Duties",body:"A **property manager** is an agent of the property owner who manages rental property. Duties include:\n- Marketing and leasing units\n- Screening and selecting tenants (in compliance with Fair Housing laws)\n- Collecting rent and managing finances\n- Maintaining and repairing the property\n- Handling tenant complaints and disputes\n- Ensuring legal compliance (landlord-tenant laws, fair housing, building codes)\n- Evicting tenants when necessary\n\n**Management Agreement:** The contract between owner and property manager. Covers:\n- Manager's authority and responsibilities\n- Fee structure (typically a percentage of collected rent, often 8-12%)\n- Term and termination provisions\n- Reporting requirements\n- Owner's obligations\n\n**Fiduciary Duty:** The property manager owes fiduciary duties to the OWNER (the principal), not to the tenants. This includes loyalty, accounting, confidentiality, and reasonable care. The manager must handle owner funds in a TRUST account, separate from the manager's own funds.\n\n**Leasing Agent:** In many states, a property manager who negotiates leases must hold a real estate license (because leasing is a real estate activity). Employees performing maintenance-only duties typically do not need a license."},{heading:"B. Types of Leases",body:"A **lease** is a contract that transfers possession (but not ownership) of real property from landlord to tenant for a specified period in exchange for rent.\n\n**Estate for Years (Tenancy for Years):** Fixed term with a specific start and end date. No notice needed to terminate \u2014 it ends automatically on the stated end date. Can be any length (even less than a year).\n\n**Periodic Estate (Periodic Tenancy):** Automatically renews at the end of each period (month-to-month, week-to-week). Continues indefinitely until either party gives proper notice. Common notice: 30 days for month-to-month.\n\n**Estate at Will:** No fixed term; either party can terminate at any time. Typically created when an owner allows someone to occupy property without a formal lease.\n\n**Estate at Sufferance:** Tenant stays after the lease expires without the landlord's permission (holdover tenant). The tenant is technically trespassing. The landlord can evict or accept rent, which may create a new periodic tenancy.\n\n**Gross Lease:** Tenant pays fixed rent; landlord pays all operating expenses (taxes, insurance, maintenance). Most common for residential.\n\n**Net Lease:** Tenant pays rent PLUS some or all operating expenses. Types: Single net (rent + taxes), Double net (rent + taxes + insurance), Triple net (rent + taxes + insurance + maintenance). Common in commercial.\n\n**Ground Lease:** Long-term lease of land only (typically 50-99 years). Tenant owns improvements on the land but not the land itself. Maryland ground rent is a form of ground lease.\n\n**Lease vs. License:** A lease transfers POSSESSION (property interest). A license is mere PERMISSION to use (no possession)."},{heading:"C. Landlord and Tenant Duties",body:"**Landlord's Duties:**\n- **Possession:** Deliver legal possession of the premises to the tenant\n- **Habitability (Implied Warranty of Habitability):** Maintain the property in a habitable condition (heat, water, electricity, structural integrity, pest-free). Cannot be waived in residential leases.\n- **Quiet Enjoyment:** Tenant has the right to possess and use the property without interference from the landlord or anyone with superior title\n- **Repairs:** Maintain common areas; make necessary repairs (except those caused by tenant negligence)\n- **Security Deposit:** Handle according to state law (separate account, interest requirements, timely return)\n\n**Tenant's Duties:**\n- **Pay Rent:** The primary obligation. Failure to pay is grounds for eviction.\n- **Maintain Premises:** Keep the unit clean and sanitary; proper use of facilities\n- **No Waste:** Not damage or destroy the property\n- **No Illegal Activity:** Not use the property for illegal purposes\n- **Access:** Allow landlord reasonable access for repairs and inspections (typically with 24-hour notice)\n\n**Constructive Eviction:** When the landlord's failure to maintain the property (or actions/inactions) makes it UNINHABITABLE, the tenant may vacate and terminate the lease without penalty. The tenant MUST actually vacate to claim constructive eviction.\n\n**Retaliatory Eviction:** A landlord cannot evict a tenant in retaliation for complaining about housing code violations or exercising legal rights. Most states (including Maryland) prohibit retaliatory eviction."},{heading:"D. Security Deposits, Eviction, and Fair Housing",body:"**Security Deposits:** Money held by landlord to cover unpaid rent, damages beyond normal wear and tear, and cleaning. Must be held in a separate account (trust/escrow). States set maximum amounts, return deadlines, and interest requirements.\n\n**Normal Wear and Tear vs. Damage:** Landlord can deduct for DAMAGE (tenant broke a window) but not for normal WEAR AND TEAR (carpet gradually worn from walking). This is a commonly litigated distinction.\n\n**Eviction (Unlawful Detainer):** Legal process to remove a tenant. Grounds: non-payment of rent, lease violation, holdover after lease expiration, illegal activity. The landlord must go through COURT \u2014 SELF-HELP EVICTION IS ILLEGAL (cannot change locks, shut off utilities, or remove the tenant's belongings).\n\n**Fair Housing in Rental:** The Fair Housing Act prohibits discrimination in rental based on race, color, religion, national origin, sex, disability, and familial status. Applies to landlords, property managers, and real estate agents. Exemptions: owner-occupied buildings with 4 or fewer units, single-family homes rented without an agent, religious organizations, and private clubs (with limitations).\n\n**ADA and Service Animals:** Landlords must make reasonable accommodations for tenants with disabilities, including allowing service animals and emotional support animals (ESA) even in \"no pets\" buildings. Cannot charge pet fees/deposits for service animals.\n\n**Rent Control:** Local ordinances capping rent increases. Not all jurisdictions have rent control. Maryland does not have statewide rent control, but some localities may have regulation."},{heading:"Maryland Supplement \u2014 Property Management",body:"**Maryland Security Deposit Law:**\n- Maximum: 2 months' rent (no statutory limit, but common practice is 1-2 months)\n- Must be held in a Maryland bank in an escrow account\n- Interest: Must be returned with simple interest at the rate set by the state (currently 1.5% annually for most of Maryland, though some counties vary)\n- Return deadline: 45 DAYS after lease termination\n- Itemized damages: Must provide written list of damages and costs within 45 days\n- Penalty: Landlord who wrongfully withholds deposit may be liable for up to 3x the deposit amount\n\n**Maryland Eviction Process:**\n- Non-payment of rent: 10-day notice to pay or quit\n- Lease violation: 30-day notice to cure or quit\n- Holdover tenant: 30-day notice for month-to-month\n- Failure to pay rent: Landlord files Complaint and Summons Against Tenant in Possession (failure to pay rent action)\n- Tenant's right of redemption: In failure-to-pay-rent cases, tenant can pay all rent, late fees, and court costs up to the eviction date and KEEP the premises\n\n**Maryland Lead Paint Registry for Rentals:** All rental properties built before 1978 must be registered with MDE and inspected for lead paint. Landlord must provide lead certificate and EPA pamphlet to tenants.\n\n**Maryland Landlord-Tenant Law:** Codified in Md. Code, Real Property Title 8. Covers lease requirements, landlord duties, tenant duties, eviction procedures, security deposits, and habitability standards. Maryland is considered a tenant-friendly state."}],keyTerms:[]},{topicNumber:10,topicName:"Practice of Real Estate",overview:"Practice of Real Estate covers the legal and regulatory framework in which real estate licensees operate. Know federal antitrust laws (Sherman Act, price-fixing, group boycotts, market allocation, tie-in arrangements), Fair Housing Act (7 protected classes, exemptions, blockbusting, steering, redlining, ADA compliance), agency duties and disclosures, advertising rules (clear and conspicuous license identification, blind ads prohibited), license law basics (licensing requirements, renewal, continuing education, reciprocity), and the National Do Not Call Registry. Maryland-specific: Maryland Real Estate Commission (MREC) structure, MD license categories and requirements, MD agency disclosure form, MD advertising rules, MD continuing education requirements, and MD Real Estate Guaranty Fund.",sections:[{heading:"A. Federal Antitrust Laws",body:"**Sherman Antitrust Act:** Federal law prohibiting restraints of trade and monopolization. Key provisions for real estate:\n\n**Price-Fixing:** Competing brokers agreeing on commission rates \u2014 PER SE illegal. Even an informal conversation suggesting 'we all charge 6%' can constitute price-fixing. Penalties include treble damages and criminal prosecution.\n\n**Group Boycott:** Agreement among competitors to refuse to deal with a particular person or company. Example: Multiple brokers agreeing not to show listings from a discount broker.\n\n**Market Allocation:** Agreement among competitors to divide markets by territory, property type, or price range. Example: 'You take the east side, I'll take the west side.' Per se illegal.\n\n**Tie-In Arrangement:** Requiring a buyer to use a specific service as a condition of sale. Example: 'You must use our title company if you want to buy this property.' May violate antitrust and RESPA.\n\nAgents must avoid even the APPEARANCE of antitrust violations. Never discuss commission rates, fees, or market division with competitors."},{heading:"B. Fair Housing Act",body:"**Seven Protected Classes (Federal):** Race, Color, Religion, National Origin, Sex (including gender identity and sexual orientation per HUD interpretation), Disability, Familial Status (families with children under 18, pregnant women).\n\n**Prohibited Actions:**\n- Refusing to sell, rent, or negotiate\n- Discriminatory terms or conditions\n- Discriminatory advertising (\"no kids,\" \"perfect for singles\")\n- Blockbusting (inducing panic selling by suggesting a protected group is moving in)\n- Steering (directing buyers to or away from areas based on protected class)\n- Redlining (refusing to lend in certain neighborhoods based on race/ethnicity)\n- Refusing reasonable accommodations for disabilities\n\n**Blockbusting (Panic Selling):** Inducing homeowners to sell by telling them that a protected class is moving into the neighborhood, causing property values to decline. ILLEGAL.\n\n**Steering:** Channeling buyers toward or away from certain neighborhoods based on their protected class. Example: showing Hispanic buyers only properties in predominantly Hispanic neighborhoods.\n\n**Redlining:** Refusing to make loans or provide insurance in certain geographic areas, historically based on racial composition. Now prohibited.\n\n**Disability Accommodations:** Landlords must allow reasonable modifications (tenant pays) and make reasonable accommodations (landlord bears some responsibility). Service animals and ESAs must be accommodated.\n\n**Design and Construction (1991+):** Multifamily buildings (4+ units) built after March 1991 must meet accessibility standards: accessible entrance, accessible common areas, usable doors, accessible routes through units.\n\n**Exemptions:**\n- Owner-occupied 4-unit or fewer buildings\n- Single-family home rented/sold without a real estate agent\n- Religious organizations (may limit to members, but not based on other protected classes)\n- Private clubs (not open to public)\n- Housing for older persons (55+ communities, 62+ communities)\n\n**NOTE:** Exemptions do NOT apply to discriminatory ADVERTISING \u2014 all advertising must comply with Fair Housing.\n\n**Enforcement:** HUD investigates complaints. Penalties: up to $21,000+ for first violation, civil lawsuits, injunctive relief."},{heading:"C. Agency and Advertising Rules",body:"**Agency Disclosure:** Most states (including Maryland) require written agency disclosure to all prospective buyers and sellers at first substantive contact. Explains: who the agent represents, types of agency (seller's agent, buyer's agent, dual agent), and the agent's duties.\n\n**Dual Agency:** One agent or brokerage representing BOTH buyer and seller in the same transaction. Requires WRITTEN informed consent from BOTH parties. Illegal in some states without explicit disclosure and consent. In Maryland, dual agency requires written consent.\n\n**Designated Agency:** A brokerage designates one agent to represent the seller and another to represent the buyer, avoiding dual agency. Both agents owe full fiduciary duties to their respective clients.\n\n**Advertising Rules:**\n- Must clearly identify the licensee's brokerage/business name (not just the agent's name)\n- \"Blind ads\" (ads that don't disclose the advertiser is a real estate licensee) are PROHIBITED\n- Cannot use misleading terms, false claims, or exaggerated property descriptions that cross into misrepresentation\n- License status must be clear and conspicuous\n- Team ads must include brokerage name\n\n**Do Not Call Registry:** National registry of phone numbers that cannot be called for telemarketing. Real estate agents must check the registry before cold-calling. Exemptions: existing clients (18 months after last transaction), active prospects (3 months after inquiry), established business relationships."},{heading:"D. Real Estate License Law",body:"**License Categories (typical, varies by state):**\n- **Salesperson:** Must be sponsored by a licensed broker; cannot operate independently\n- **Broker:** May operate independently, employ salespeople, and manage a brokerage\n- **Associate Broker:** Licensed as a broker but works under another broker\n\n**Licensing Requirements (typical):**\n- Minimum age (18 in most states, including Maryland)\n- High school diploma or equivalent\n- Complete pre-licensing education (Maryland: 60 hours)\n- Pass state exam\n- Background check / fingerprinting\n- Find a sponsoring broker (for salesperson)\n- Errors and omissions (E&O) insurance (varies by state)\n\n**Continuing Education:** Must complete CE hours to renew license (varies by state; MD: 15 hours every 2 years including 3 hours ethics).\n\n**License Portability/Reciprocity:** Some states recognize licenses from other states. Maryland has reciprocity agreements with certain states (not all \u2014 must check MREC).\n\n**Grounds for Discipline:** Misrepresentation, fraud, commingling funds, failure to disclose, criminal conviction, violating license law. Penalties: fines, suspension, revocation, restitution."},{heading:"Maryland Supplement \u2014 Practice of Real Estate",body:"**Maryland Real Estate Commission (MREC):** The state regulatory body governing real estate licensees. Sets licensing requirements, approves education providers, investigates complaints, and disciplines licensees. Operates under the Maryland Department of Labor.\n\n**Maryland License Requirements:**\n- Salesperson: 60 hours pre-licensing education, pass state exam, find sponsoring broker, 18+ years old, high school diploma or equivalent\n- Broker: 3 years active salesperson experience, 135 hours additional education, pass broker exam\n- Continuing Education: 15 hours every 2 years, including 3 hours ethics, 1.5 hours fair housing, and 1.5 hours agency\n\n**Maryland Agency Disclosure:** Maryland requires written agency disclosure form \"Understanding Whom Real Estate Agents Represent\" to be presented at first scheduled face-to-face contact with a prospective buyer or seller. The form explains: seller's agent, buyer's agent, dual agent, and prescribed duties. Agent and consumer must sign and date.\n\n**Maryland Advertising Rules:** All ads must include the name of the LICENSED BROKERAGE (not just the agent or team name). The brokerage name must be displayed in a reasonably prominent manner. MD Home Improvement Commission license number must appear for new home sales ads.\n\n**Maryland Real Estate Guaranty Fund:** Provides reimbursement to consumers who suffer financial loss due to a licensee's misconduct (fraud, embezzlement, conversion of funds). Funded by license fees. Maximum recovery: $25,000 per claim, $50,000 per licensee aggregate.\n\n**Maryland Fair Housing:** Maryland adds MARITAL STATUS to the federal protected classes. The Maryland Commission on Civil Rights enforces state fair housing laws."}],keyTerms:[]},{topicNumber:11,topicName:"Real Estate Calculations",overview:"Real Estate Calculations is tested throughout the PSI exam \u2014 both as standalone math questions and embedded in financing, valuation, and closing problems. Master: proration calculations (365-day and 360-day methods), property tax calculations (assessed value, mill rate), commission calculations (split commissions), loan calculations (LTV, points, interest), seller net proceeds, transfer/recordation taxes, acreage and square footage math, and appreciation/depreciation. Maryland-specific: Maryland transfer tax (0.5% state) and recordation tax calculations (per $500 of consideration).",sections:[{heading:"A. Prorations",body:"**Proration:** Dividing expenses (property taxes, HOA dues, insurance, rent) between buyer and seller based on the portion of the period each party owns the property. Prorations appear on the settlement statement.\n\n**365-Day Method (Actual Day):** Uses the actual number of days in the year. The daily rate = annual amount / 365. Multiply by the number of days each party is responsible for.\n\n**360-Day Method (Statutory/Banker's Year):** Uses a 360-day year and 30-day months. Daily rate = annual amount / 360.\n\n**Property Tax Proration:** The most commonly tested proration. Property taxes are typically paid in ARREARS (for the prior year) or in ADVANCE, depending on state/local practice.\n\n**Proration Rule:** The party who OWNS the property on the closing date is responsible for that day's expenses.\n\n**Proration at Closing Example:**\nAnnual property taxes: $3,650. Closing date: July 15. Seller has paid taxes through December 31 (paid in advance for the full year). Buyer must reimburse seller for the remaining 169 days (July 16 - Dec 31).\n- 365-day method: $3,650 / 365 = $10/day. $10 x 169 days = $1,690 buyer credit to seller.\n\n**Accrued vs. Prepaid Items:**\n- **Accrued (Arrears):** Seller owes for time they owned but haven't paid yet \u2192 seller DEBIT at closing\n- **Prepaid:** Seller has already paid for time the buyer will own \u2192 BUYER DEBIT (reimburses seller)"},{heading:"B. Property Tax Calculations",body:"**Assessed Value:** The value assigned by the tax assessor for property tax purposes. May be a percentage of market value (assessment ratio) or 100% of market value (as in Maryland).\n\n**Assessment Ratio:** Applied to market value to determine assessed value. Example: 30% assessment ratio on $300,000 market value = $90,000 assessed value.\n\n**Mill Rate (Millage Rate):** The tax rate expressed in mills. One mill = $1 of tax per $1,000 of assessed value = 0.001.\n\n**Mill Rate Calculation:** Mills / 1,000 x Assessed Value = Annual tax.\n\nExample: 20 mills on $200,000 assessed value = 20/1,000 x $200,000 = 0.020 x $200,000 = $4,000 annual tax.\n\nOr: 20 mills = $20 per $1,000. $200,000 / $1,000 = 200. 200 x $20 = $4,000.\n\n**Tax Rate per $100:** Some jurisdictions express tax rates per $100 of assessed value. Example: $2.50 per $100 on $200,000 assessed value = $200,000 / $100 = 2,000. 2,000 x $2.50 = $5,000.\n\n**Special Assessments:** Additional taxes for specific improvements (sidewalks, sewers, street lights) that benefit the property. Separate from regular property taxes.\n\n**Property Tax Formula Summary:**\n1. Market Value x Assessment Ratio = Assessed Value\n2. Assessed Value x Mill Rate (/1,000) = Annual Tax\n3. Annual Tax / Payment Frequency = Installment Amount"},{heading:"C. Commission Calculations",body:"**Commission Rate:** Typically a percentage of the sale price (or gross lease value for commercial). No standard rate \u2014 commissions are always NEGOTIABLE (any suggestion of a standard rate raises antitrust concerns).\n\n**Commission Split:** The commission is divided between the listing brokerage and selling brokerage, and then split between broker and agent per their agreement.\n\n**Commission Formula:**\n1. Sale Price x Commission Rate = Total Commission\n2. Total Commission x Split % = Brokerage's share\n3. Brokerage's Share x Agent Split % = Agent's commission\n\n**Example:**\n- Sale price: $400,000\n- Commission: 6%\n- Listing brokerage gets 3%, selling brokerage gets 3%\n- Agent split with broker: 70/30\n\nTotal commission = $400,000 x 0.06 = $24,000\nListing brokerage share = $24,000 x 0.5 = $12,000\nListing agent commission = $12,000 x 0.70 = $8,400\n\n**Seller Net Proceeds:**\n1. Sale Price\n2. - Mortgage payoff(s)\n3. - Commission\n4. - Transfer taxes and recordation\n5. - Other closing costs (attorney fees, title insurance, etc.)\n6. - Prorated items (seller debits)\n7. + Prorated items (seller credits)\n8. = Seller Net Proceeds"},{heading:"D. Loan and Finance Calculations",body:"**Loan-to-Value (LTV):** Loan Amount / Property Value (lower of sales price or appraised value).\n- LTV > 80% \u2192 PMI required on conventional loans\n- Value = Loan / LTV\n- Loan = Value x LTV\n\n**Discount Points:** 1 point = 1% of the LOAN amount (not the purchase price). Paid at closing to permanently reduce the interest rate.\n- Points cost = Loan Amount x Points %\n\n**Interest Calculations:** Annual interest (first year approximation) = Loan Amount x Interest Rate.\n\n**PITI Payment:** Principal + Interest + Taxes + Insurance = Total monthly housing payment.\n\n**Debt-to-Income Ratios:**\n- Front-end (Housing Ratio) = Monthly housing expense / Gross monthly income\n- Back-end (DTI) = Total monthly debt / Gross monthly income\n\n**Amortization:** Monthly payment remains constant (fixed rate); interest portion decreases, principal portion increases over time."},{heading:"E. Area, Acreage, and Measurement Calculations",body:"**Must Memorize:**\n- 1 acre = 43,560 square feet\n- 1 mile = 5,280 linear feet\n- 1 square mile = 640 acres\n- 1 hectare \u2248 2.47 acres\n- 1 square yard = 9 square feet\n\n**Area Formulas:**\n- Rectangle: Length x Width\n- Square: Side x Side\n- Triangle: 1/2 x Base x Height\n- Irregular shapes: break into rectangles and triangles\n\n**Volume:** Length x Width x Height (cubic feet, cubic yards). Used for excavation, concrete, fill dirt.\n\n**Frontage:** The width of a property along a street, highway, or waterway.\n\n**Price per Square Foot:** Sale Price / Square Footage.\n\n**Perimeter:** Sum of all sides. For rectangle: 2 x (Length + Width)."},{heading:"F. Appreciation and Depreciation",body:"**Appreciation:** Increase in property value over time.\n- Future Value = Current Value x (1 + Appreciation Rate)^Years\n\n**Depreciation:** Decrease in property value over time.\n- Future Value = Current Value x (1 - Depreciation Rate)^Years\n\n**Simple (Non-Compounding) Approximation:** Current Value x Rate x Years = Change in value (for exam purposes when not specified as compounding).\n\n**Example:** $250,000 home appreciates 4% per year for 3 years. Future value \u2248 $250,000 x 1.04 x 1.04 x 1.04 = $250,000 x 1.125 = $281,250.\n\nOr simple: $250,000 x 4% x 3 years = $30,000 increase. $250,000 + $30,000 = $280,000."},{heading:"Maryland Supplement \u2014 Calculations",body:"**Maryland State Transfer Tax:** 0.5% of the consideration (purchase price). Example: $400,000 sale \u2192 $400,000 x 0.005 = $2,000 state transfer tax. Typically split between buyer and seller per contract, but customarily seller pays.\n\n**Maryland County Transfer Tax:** Varies by county (typically 0.5% to 1.5%). Example: Baltimore County charges 1.5% local transfer tax. Combined state + county transfer taxes can be significant in MD.\n\n**Maryland Recordation Tax:** Tax on recording deeds and mortgages. For deeds: $3.50 to $5.00 per $500 of consideration (varies by county). Round UP to the nearest $500. Example: $400,000 purchase, $4.00 per $500 rate. $400,000 / $500 = 800 increments. 800 x $4.00 = $3,200 recordation tax.\n\n**Example Combined:** $400,000 home in Baltimore County:\n- State transfer tax: $400,000 x 0.5% = $2,000\n- County transfer tax: $400,000 x 1.5% = $6,000\n- Recordation tax (deed): $400,000 / $500 = 800 x $4.00 = $3,200\n- Total transfer/recording fees: $11,200\n\n**Maryland Property Tax:** Based on 100% of assessed value (fair market value). Mill rates vary by county. Example: 25 mills on $300,000 assessed value = 25/1,000 x $300,000 = $7,500."}],keyTerms:[]},{topicNumber:12,topicName:"Maryland State Law",overview:"Maryland State Law accounts for approximately 12% of the PSI exam \u2014 the state-specific portion. This topic covers the Maryland Real Estate Commission (MREC), license categories and requirements (salesperson, broker), pre-licensing and continuing education (NEW CE requirements effective Oct 1, 2025), the Maryland agency disclosure form 'Understanding Whom Real Estate Agents Represent,' Maryland-specific contract and disclosure requirements, the Maryland Real Estate Guaranty Fund, Maryland landlord-tenant law, Maryland fair housing (adds marital status), Maryland property tax and transfer/recordation taxes, Maryland environmental protections (Chesapeake Bay Critical Area Act), and Maryland ground rent. This is the most research-intensive topic \u2014 many PSI state-law questions come from these Maryland-specific rules.",sections:[{heading:"A. Maryland Real Estate Commission (MREC)",body:"The **Maryland Real Estate Commission (MREC)** is the state regulatory body governing real estate licensees under the Maryland Department of Labor, Division of Occupational and Professional Licensing.\n\n**MREC Responsibilities:**\n- Sets licensing standards and approves pre-licensing and CE education providers\n- Administers the state licensing exam (through PSI)\n- Issues, renews, and disciplines real estate licenses\n- Investigates consumer complaints against licensees\n- Maintains the Real Estate Guaranty Fund\n- Publishes and updates required disclosure forms\n\n**MREC Composition:** Commissioners appointed by the Governor. Includes licensed real estate professionals and consumer members."},{heading:"B. Maryland License Categories and Requirements",body:"### Salesperson License Requirements\n- **Age:** 18+ years old\n- **Education:** High school diploma or equivalent\n- **Pre-Licensing:** 60 clock hours from an MREC-approved education provider (must include 3 hours of real estate ethics)\n- **Exam:** Pass both national and Maryland state portions of the PSI exam\n- **Broker Affiliation:** Must have a commitment from a licensed Maryland broker to affiliate\n- **Application Fee:** $90\n- **License Term:** 2 years\n- **Post-Exam Window:** Must apply within 1 year of passing the exam\n- **Affiliation Window:** If license is issued on inactive status, licensee has 3 years to affiliate with a brokerage\n\n### Broker License Requirements\n- **Experience:** Must have held an ACTIVE real estate license for at least 3 consecutive years immediately prior to application (no breaks, inactive periods, late renewals, or suspensions)\n- **Pre-Licensing Education:** 135 clock hours from an MREC-approved provider\n- **Exam:** Pass the PSI broker exam\n\n### Associate Broker\n- Licensed as a broker but works under another broker. Same CE requirements as a salesperson.\n\n### Branch Office Manager\n- Licensed broker managing a branch office. Additional supervision CE required."},{heading:"C. Maryland Continuing Education (NEW \u2014 Effective October 1, 2025)",body:"All Maryland licensees must complete **15 hours of CE every 2-year renewal period**. CE must be completed at least 30 days before submitting a renewal application.\n\n### First-Renewal Salespersons (licensed on/after Oct 1, 2023):\nMandatory topic-specific hours (cannot substitute):\n- Contracts: 3 hours\n- Principles of Brokerage Relationships & Disclosure: 3 hours\n- Principles of Real Estate Practice: 3 hours\n- Property Management & Landlord/Tenant Laws: 3 hours\n- Professionalism: 1.5 hours\n- Real Estate Financing: 1.5 hours\n- **Total: 15 hours**\n\n### All Other Salespersons / Associate Brokers (Residential):\n- Legal & Legislative Update (Topic A): 3 hours\n- Fair Housing (Topic C): 2 hours\n- Ethics (Topic D): 3 hours\n- Electives: 4 hours\n- Principles of Brokerage Relationships & Disclosure (Topic H): 3 hours\n- **Total: 15 hours**\n\n### Brokers / Branch Office Managers / Team Leaders (Residential):\n- Same as above except: 2.5 elective hours + 1.5-hour Supervision course (Topic I). New supervisors have 90 days to complete the Supervision course.\n\n### Key Rules:\n- MD-specific Topics A, D, and H may NOT be substituted with out-of-state courses\n- CE must be completed during the license term\n- Late CE completion is not accepted for renewal"},{heading:"D. Maryland Agency Disclosure",body:"**Understanding Whom Real Estate Agents Represent:** Maryland's mandatory written agency disclosure form. Must be provided to all prospective buyers and sellers.\n\n**When Must It Be Provided?**\n- At the **first scheduled face-to-face contact** with an unrepresented party\n- If the first contact is NOT face-to-face (phone, email, text): the agent must disclose who they represent through that SAME medium, then provide the written form at the first scheduled face-to-face meeting\n- **Open Houses:** Instead of providing the form to every attendee, the agent may post the Open House Notice (MREC form) in a conspicuous place \u2014 at least 8.5\" x 11\", in color\n- If the unrepresented party REFUSES to sign: the licensee may self-certify on the form that disclosure was made\n\n**What the Form Discloses:**\n1. **Seller's Agent:** Works exclusively for the seller. May assist buyer but owes loyalty only to seller.\n2. **Subagent:** Licensee from a different company who has a brokerage relationship with the seller and assists a buyer in a non-brokerage capacity. Loyalty is to the seller.\n3. **Buyer's Agent:** Represents the buyer under a WRITTEN brokerage agreement.\n4. **Dual Agent:** When buyer's and seller's agent work for the SAME brokerage. Both parties must sign a Consent for Dual Agency form. Intra-company agents are assigned to each party.\n\n**Key Consumer Rights Disclosed:**\n- Agents must treat all parties honestly and fairly\n- Agents must disclose ALL material facts about a property (confidentiality does not apply to material facts)\n- All agreements must be in writing\n- The form is NOT a contract \u2014 it creates no obligations on the consumer\n\n**Legal Basis:** Md. Brokers Act \xa717-530."},{heading:"E. Maryland Real Estate Guaranty Fund",body:"The **Maryland Real Estate Guaranty Fund** provides reimbursement to consumers who suffer financial loss due to a licensee's misconduct in a real estate transaction.\n\n**Covered Acts:** Fraud, embezzlement, conversion of funds, mishandling of trust money, and other dishonest or illegal acts by a licensed real estate agent or broker acting in their capacity as a licensee.\n\n**Funding:** Funded by license fees paid by all Maryland real estate licensees \u2014 not by taxpayer money.\n\n**Recovery Limits:**\n- **Per Claim:** Maximum $25,000\n- **Per Licensee (Aggregate):** Maximum $50,000 per licensee for all claims combined\n\n**Claim Process:**\n- Consumer must first obtain a final judgment against the licensee and exhaust collection efforts\n- Then apply to the MREC for reimbursement from the Guaranty Fund\n- MREC investigates and determines eligibility\n\n**Not Covered:**\n- Disputes over commission amounts\n- Negligence that does not involve dishonest or illegal acts\n- Claims against unlicensed individuals\n- Business/investment losses not directly related to licensee misconduct"},{heading:"F. Maryland Fair Housing and Civil Rights",body:"**Maryland Fair Housing Law:** Maryland adds MARITAL STATUS, SEXUAL ORIENTATION, and GENDER IDENTITY as protected classes, making 10 total protected classes under state law (the 7 federal classes plus these 3 Maryland additions).\n\n**Enforcement:** The Maryland Commission on Civil Rights enforces state fair housing laws. Complaints must be filed within 1 year of the alleged discriminatory act.\n\n**Maryland-Specific Provisions:**\n- Prohibits discrimination in housing based on marital status\n- Prohibits discrimination based on sexual orientation and gender identity (explicitly codified, not just HUD interpretation)\n- Local jurisdictions (Montgomery County, Howard County, Baltimore City, etc.) may add additional protected classes such as source of income\n\n**Disability Accommodations:** Maryland follows federal standards for reasonable accommodations and modifications. Service animals and emotional support animals must be accommodated with proper documentation."},{heading:"G. Maryland Landlord-Tenant Law",body:"Codified in **Md. Code, Real Property Title 8**. Maryland is considered a tenant-friendly state.\n\n**Security Deposits:**\n- Must be held in a Maryland bank in an escrow account (separate from landlord's funds)\n- Must earn simple interest (rate set by state, currently 1.5% annually for most of Maryland)\n- Return deadline: **45 days** after lease termination\n- Must provide written itemized list of damages and costs within 45 days\n- Wrongful withholding penalty: Up to **3 times** the deposit amount\n\n**Eviction Process:**\n- Non-payment of rent: **10-day notice** to pay or quit\n- Lease violation: **30-day notice** to cure or quit\n- Holdover tenant: 30-day notice for month-to-month tenancy\n- Filed as: Complaint and Summons Against Tenant in Possession (failure to pay rent action)\n\n**Tenant Right of Redemption:** In failure-to-pay-rent cases, the tenant can pay ALL rent, late fees, and court costs up to the eviction date and KEEP the premises. This is a significant tenant protection in Maryland.\n\n**Lead Paint Registration:** All Maryland rental properties built before 1978 must be registered with MDE and inspected for lead paint. Landlord must provide lead certificate and EPA pamphlet to tenants.\n\n**Warranty of Habitability:** Implied in all residential leases. Landlord must maintain heat, water, electricity, structural integrity, and pest-free conditions. Cannot be waived.\n\n**Retaliatory Eviction:** Prohibited. A landlord cannot evict a tenant for complaining about code violations or exercising legal rights."},{heading:"H. Maryland Property Taxes, Transfer Taxes, and Recordation",body:"**Property Tax Assessment:**\n- Maryland assesses property at **100% of fair market value**\n- Reassessment every **3 years** on a county rotation\n- Appeals: Property Tax Assessment Appeal Board (PTAAB), then Maryland Tax Court\n\n**Homestead Property Tax Credit:** Caps annual assessment increases for primary residences (varies by county, typically 10% or less for state portion). Protects homeowners from rapid tax increases due to appreciation.\n\n**State Transfer Tax:** **0.5%** of the consideration (purchase price). Customarily paid by seller but negotiable.\n\n**County Transfer Tax:** Varies by county, typically 0.5% to 1.5%. Paid in addition to state transfer tax.\n\n**Recordation Tax:** Tax on recording deeds and security instruments in county land records:\n- Rate: $3.50 to $5.00 per $500 of consideration (varies by county)\n- Round UP to the nearest $500 when calculating increments\n- Applies to both deeds and mortgages\n\n**Example:** $400,000 home in a county with $4.00/$500 recordation rate:\n- $400,000 / $500 = 800 increments (round up if needed)\n- 800 x $4.00 = $3,200 recordation tax\n\n**Total Transfer/Recording Taxes (Example):**\n- State transfer: $400,000 x 0.5% = $2,000\n- County transfer: $400,000 x 1.5% = $6,000 (varies)\n- Recordation: $3,200\n- Combined: $11,200 \u2014 significant closing cost"},{heading:"I. Maryland Environmental and Land Use Laws",body:"**Chesapeake Bay Critical Area Act:** Protects land within **1,000 feet** of the Chesapeake Bay and its tidal tributaries. Imposes:\n- Development density limits\n- Buffer requirements (typically 100-foot vegetated buffer from water)\n- Impervious surface caps\n- Habitat protection requirements\n\nAffects property use and development in many Maryland counties including Anne Arundel, Baltimore, Calvert, Cecil, Charles, Dorchester, Harford, Kent, Prince George's, Queen Anne's, St. Mary's, Somerset, Talbot, Wicomico, and Worcester.\n\n**Maryland Department of the Environment (MDE):** Enforces state environmental laws:\n- Lead paint abatement and registration\n- Asbestos regulation\n- Underground storage tank (UST) management\n- Water quality and wetlands protection\n\n**Maryland Lead Paint Registry:** Rental properties built before 1978 must be REGISTERED with MDE and INSPECTED. Landlords must provide lead certificate and EPA pamphlet to tenants. Maryland's requirements are STRICTER than federal law.\n\n**Maryland Homeowner's Association Act:** Regulates HOAs; requires disclosure of HOA documents to buyers; limits what HOAs can restrict (e.g., cannot prohibit solar panels in most cases, cannot restrict political signs near election time).\n\n**Maryland Condominium Act:** Governs condo creation, sales, and operations. Requires public offering statement for new condo sales. Buyer has **15-day right to cancel** after receiving the offering statement."},{heading:"J. Maryland Ground Rent and Unique Property Forms",body:"**Maryland Ground Rent:** A unique Maryland property form where a homeowner owns the improvements (house/building) but LEASES the land under a long-term ground lease (typically 99 years, renewable forever). The ground rent holder owns the land.\n\n**Key Ground Rent Facts:**\n- Most common in Baltimore City and older Maryland communities\n- Ground rent is typically a nominal amount (often established decades ago at low fixed rates)\n- Maryland law gives the homeowner the **right to REDEEM** (buy out) the ground rent\n- Redemption price is determined by capitalizing the annual ground rent at a statutory rate\n- A ground rent tenant who fails to pay can be sued but generally CANNOT BE EVICTED solely for non-payment of ground rent\n- Ground rent must be registered with the state; unregistered ground rent may be extinguished\n\n**Maryland Tenancy by the Entirety:** Recognized for married couples. Key features:\n- Neither spouse can unilaterally sell, convey, or encumber the property\n- Protects property from INDIVIDUAL creditors of one spouse (but not joint creditors)\n- Includes right of survivorship\n- Divorce converts tenancy by entirety to tenancy in common"},{heading:"K. Additional Maryland Laws and Regulations",body:"**Earnest Money Deposits:** Maryland law requires that earnest money deposits be deposited into the broker's escrow account within **7 business days** of contract acceptance. Failure to timely deposit may result in disciplinary action by MREC.\n\n**Security Deposit Maximum:** Maryland limits security deposits to a maximum of **2 months' rent** for unfurnished units (Md. Real Property \xa78-203). Deposits must be held in a Maryland financial institution in an escrow account. Landlords must pay interest on security deposits held for more than 6 months (currently at the statutory rate).\n\n**Broker Record Retention:** Maryland brokers must maintain and preserve all transaction records for a minimum of **5 years** from the date of closing or termination of the transaction. This includes contracts, agency disclosure forms, closing statements, and correspondence. Records must be available for MREC inspection.\n\n**Advertising Rules:** All real estate advertising by a licensed broker or salesperson must include the **licensed name of the broker** (not just the salesperson's name). Team advertising must include the broker's name. Blind ads (advertisements without brokerage identification) are prohibited. MREC may impose fines for advertising violations.\n\n**Domestic Violence Lease Termination:** Under Maryland law (Md. Real Property \xa78-5A-01 et seq.), a tenant who is a victim of domestic violence, sexual assault, or stalking may **terminate the lease early** with 30 days' written notice. The tenant must provide documentation (protective order, peace order, or statement from a qualified professional). The tenant is responsible for rent only through the termination date.\n\n**Anti-Retaliation Protection:** Maryland prohibits a landlord from retaliating against a tenant who files a complaint about housing conditions, joins a tenant organization, or exercises legal rights. Retaliatory actions (eviction, rent increase, service reduction) within **6 months** of the tenant's protected activity are presumed retaliatory. The landlord bears the burden of proving otherwise.\n\n**Maryland Consumer Protection Act (MCPA):** The MCPA (Md. Commercial Law \xa713-101 et seq.) applies to real estate transactions. It prohibits unfair, abusive, or deceptive trade practices. Licensees violating the MCPA may face civil penalties, actual damages to consumers, and MREC discipline. The MCPA provides a private right of action for consumers.\n\n**HOA Super Lien:** Under Maryland law, a homeowners association (HOA) lien for unpaid assessments has **super-lien** status \u2014 limited priority over a first mortgage for up to **4 months** of regular assessments (not including late fees, fines, or interest). After foreclosure, the HOA may recover the remaining balance from the former owner but it loses priority over the first mortgage.\n\n**Foreclosure Mediation:** Maryland provides foreclosure mediation for **owner-occupied residential property**. Before a foreclosure sale can proceed, the lender must send a Notice of Intent to Foreclose at least 45 days before filing. The homeowner may request mediation, which stays the foreclosure process. Mediation is conducted through the Maryland Office of Administrative Hearings."}],keyTerms:[]}]},238,[]);
